{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-96f9d39fe936c386", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tPAGES=21\n2\t=== PAGE 1 ===\n3\tJoint Text-Audio Alignment for EEG-to-Text Decoding\n4\tin Chinese Speech Production and Perception\n5\tTian Zheng*1,2, Xurong Xie2, Xinxin Zhu3, Xiaolan Peng2, and Feng Tian2\n6\t1University of the Chinese Academy of Sciences\n7\t2Institute of Software, Chinese Academy of Sciences\n8\t3Beijing Language and Culture University\n9\t*E-mail: zhengtian24@mails.ucas.ac.cn\n10\tJuly 29, 2026\n11\tAbstract\n12\tDecodingspeechinformationdirectlyfromscalpelectroencephalography(EEG)into\n13\ttext provides a potential non-invasive neural communication pathway for individuals\n14\twith severe speech and motor impairments. Compared with invasive approaches such\n15\tas electrocorticography, EEG is safer and more widely deployable, but it remains\n16\tsubstantially more challenging to decode. This challenge is exacerbated in Chinese\n17\tsentence decoding, which must handle a high-dimensional output space with thousands\n18\tof characters, severe inter-subject variability, and low signal-to-noise ratios for text\n19\talignment. Existingmethodscommittoasinglesupervisoryaxis—eithertextsemantics\n20\tor audio acoustic features—yet neither can simultaneously satisfy the demands of\n21\tsentence-level discriminability and fine-grained temporal resolution required for large-\n22\tvocabulary Chinese decoding. We introduce EEGAlign, a novel parameter-efficient\n23\tframework that jointly aligns EEG along two axes: text alignment with BGE-M3 text\n24\tembeddings and audio alignment with wav2vec 2.0 speech features via contrastive\n25\tlearning, followed by CTC character-sequence decoding. On ChineseEEG-2 data,\n26\tEEGAlignyieldsstate-of-the-artclosed-setsentenceclassificationperformance,reaching\n27\tup to 82.37% Top-1 accuracy on Reading Aloud EEG and 41.43% on Passive Listening\n28\tEEGoutof101candidates.Ablationstudiesshowthatthetwoalignmentaxesarehighly\n29\tcomplementary: combining them yields consistently better performance than either\n30\talone. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study to decode large-vocabulary\n31\tChinesesentencesfromnon-invasiveEEGduringovertspeechproductionwhileachieving\n32\tstrong classification performance in a relatively large closed-set candidate-sentence\n33\tsetting.\n34\t1 Introduction\n35\tFor individuals whose speech and motor functions are severely impaired by conditions\n36\tsuch as locked-in syndrome, ALS, or stroke, brain–computer interfaces (BCIs) that decode\n37\tspeech intentions from neural activity offer a vital route toward restoring communication.\n38\tWhile invasive recordings have demonstrated high-performance decoding [1], the necessity of\n39\tsurgical implantation limits their broad clinical deployment. Scalp electroencephalography\n40\t(EEG) presents a non-invasive, accessible, and safe alternative, yet it must overcome a\n41\tsignificant “quality gap” characterized by low spatial resolution and three primary technical\n42\thurdles. First, signal weakness requires extracting speech and language activity from noisy\n43\trecordings affected by artifacts and background neural noise. Second, subject variability\n44\tin cortical anatomy and neurophysiology creates distribution shifts that hinder model\n45\t1\n46\t6202\n47\tluJ\n48\t82\n49\t]IA.sc[\n50\t1v62652.7062:viXra\n51\t=== PAGE 2 ===\n52\tgeneralization [2, 3]. Finally, output-space complexity makes mapping neural representations\n53\tto discrete tokens particularly difficult. These issues are further exacerbated in Chinese\n54\tsentence-level decoding, making it a major challenge for translational neural decoding.\n55\tRecent progress in EEG-to-text has addressed these challenges along three empirical\n56\tlines, though most advances target passive listening or silent reading and evaluate open-\n57\tended generation metrics (BLEU/ROUGE) rather than exact-match accuracy. The first\n58\tline integrates pretrained language models [4–6] or scales to LLM backbones with semantic\n59\treranking [7–9]. While these methods gain sentence-level discriminability from powerful\n60\ttext priors, they share a critical limitation: alignment is purely semantic, providing no fine-\n61\tgrained temporal supervision—and their autoregressive decoders are prone to hallucination\n62\twhen EEG input is noisy. The second line reconstructs acoustic intermediates such as\n63\tmel-spectrograms [10–12], motivated by the observation that direct text discretization\n64\tdiscards prosodic structure still reflected in neural activity. These systems capture temporal\n65\tdynamicsbuttreatacousticreconstructionasthemaingoalandrarelyconsidersentence-level\n66\tdiscriminability. The third line builds foundation-scale EEG encoders via self-supervised\n67\tpretraining [13, 14], offering generic representations that still require task-specific alignment\n68\tfor downstream decoding. Crucially, all three paradigms have been driven primarily by\n69\tnon-Chinese datasets (e.g., ZuCo [15]), and none jointly exploits text and audio supervision.\n70\tThe few studies investigating overt speech production typically restrict decoding to\n71\tisolated syllables or words [16, 17]. EEG-to-text decoding remains largely unexplored for\n72\tcontinuous active speech; we found only one preprint [18] using 175 hours of data and\n73\treporting relatively weak classification. In this setting, both articulation-induced motor\n74\tsignals and linguistic representations are crucial for decoding. ChineseEEG-2 [19] provides\n75\tthe first Chinese continuous speech EEG dataset with matched Reading Aloud (RA) and\n76\tPassive Listening (PL) paradigms. Yet no existing method jointly exploits text and audio\n77\tsupervisiononthisdata: textsemanticalignmentalonelacksfine-grainedtemporalresolution,\n78\twhileaudioacousticalignmentaloneoffersweakinter-sentenceseparability. Combiningthem\n79\taddresses complementary granularities of speech and language representation.\n80\tBuildingonthisobservation,weproposeEEGAlign,aparameter-efficientframeworkthat\n81\tjointly optimizes an EEG encoder with text alignment (to frozen BGE-M3 text embeddings)\n82\tand audio alignment (to wav2vec 2.0 speech features) via contrastive learning alongside CTC\n83\tcharacter-sequence decoding. Lightweight bottleneck adapters (∼16K params per subject)\n84\thandle inter-subject variability, and a three-stage curriculum stabilizes multi-objective\n85\toptimization. Experiments conducted on ChineseEEG-2 demonstrate the effectiveness of\n86\tEEGAlign. The framework is backbone-agnostic: both a 9.91M-parameter Conformer and\n87\ta pretrained LaBraM encoder [14] achieve strong performance, confirming that gains stem\n88\tfrom the alignment formulation. Our contributions are: (i) we propose EEGAlign as a\n89\tprincipled combination of complementary text-semantic and speech-acoustic supervision\n90\tfor EEG-to-text decoding in speech production and perception; (ii) EEGAlign achieves\n91\tstate-of-the-art closed-set sentence classification performance on ChineseEEG-2, reaching up\n92\tto 82.37% on RA and 41.43% on PL (101 candidates); and (iii) we systematically analyze\n93\tthe alignment mechanisms through ablations, representational geometry, and candidate-set\n94\tsensitivity. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study to decode large-vocabulary\n95\tChinese sentences from non-invasive EEG during overt speech production while achieving\n96\tstrong classification performance in a relatively large closed-set candidate-sentence setting.\n97\t2 Related work\n98\tNon-invasive EEG decoding has transitioned from rudimentary discrete unit classification\n99\ttowardcontinuouslanguagereconstructionandretrieval. Currentfrontiersinthefieldfocuson\n100\tdecoding continuous language directly from neural signals, with technical evolution primarily\n101\tbifurcating into two trajectories: acoustic reconstruction and cross-modal alignment.\n102\tEarly investigations established the feasibility of linking non-invasive brain recordings\n103\t2\n104\t=== PAGE 3 ===\n105\tto continuous speech via contrastive learning between speech representations and neural\n106\tactivity [20]. Subsequent research has focused on the fine-grained reconstruction of acoustic\n107\tfeatures, such as mel-spectrograms, by refining sequence modeling through State Space\n108\tModels (SSMs) and dynamic multiscale fusion [10, 12, 11]. However, these methods center\n109\ton acoustic fidelity, which introduces an objective mismatch with the ultimate goal of text\n110\tidentification. Furthermore, they often lack explicit sentence-level semantic alignment. Con-\n111\tversely, another research path aims to establish direct associations between EEG signals and\n112\ttext or semantic representations. This is achieved through multi-task contrastive learning [7],\n113\tCLIP-style architectures [21], or quantization-based discretization for language model inte-\n114\tgration [4]. Notably, Wang et al. [22] demonstrated in a match-mismatch classification task\n115\tthat the simultaneous utilization of self-supervised speech representations and contextual\n116\ttext embeddings effectively enhances performance. This provides preliminary evidence for\n117\tthe complementary value of multimodal (acoustic and semantic) features. Nevertheless, a\n118\tfundamental disparity exists between classification tasks and continuous sentence decoding:\n119\twhile the former merely identifies correspondences between signals, the latter requires the\n120\treconstruction of precise, coherent linguistic sequences from inherently noisy and ambiguous\n121\tneural activity.\n122\tTheaforementionedprogresshasbeenpredominantlydrivenbyEnglishbenchmarks. The\n123\ttonalnatureofMandarinandthescarcityofcontinuousspoken-EEGcorporaposesignificantly\n124\tgreater challenges for sentence-level decoding. Current non-invasive Chinese spoken-EEG\n125\tresearch remains confined to sub-word units, such as monosyllabic classification [16] or\n126\tpinyin-level production [17]. Consequently, decoding large-vocabulary continuous Chinese\n127\tsentences remains a research gap.\n128\tRecent advancements in representation learning and subject adaptation provide the\n129\ttechnical bedrock for addressing these complexities. Large-scale EEG foundation models,\n130\tsuch as BIOT [13] and LaBraM [14], have demonstrated that self-supervised pretraining can\n131\tyield robust, transferable neural representations. For cross-modal bridging, InfoNCE-style\n132\tcontrastive learning has emerged as the dominant approach [23]. To mitigate severe inter-\n133\tsubject variability, Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) techniques, such as LoRA [24],\n134\thave been widely adopted.\n135\t3 Method\n136\t3.1 Problem formulation and CTC-based decoder\n137\tGiven a multichannel EEG recording X ∈ RC×T acquired at 250 Hz from C = 128 scalp\n138\telectrodesoveratemporalwindowoflengthT ≤750,ourgoalistodecodethecorresponding\n139\tChinese character sequence y =(y ,y ,...,y ), where each y is one Chinese character and\n140\t1 2 L i\n141\tL is the number of characters in the target sentence. Here T denotes the number of EEG\n142\ttime samples, whereas L denotes the output length; the two are not assumed to match, and\n143\ttheir alignment is handled by CTC. This setting is highly challenging due to the extremely\n144\tlarge output space, severe inter-subject variability, and the intrinsically weak alignment\n145\tbetween scalp EEG and discrete text tokens.\n146\tCurrent approaches to EEG-to-text decoding generally fall into two paradigms: autore-\n147\tgressive sequence-to-sequence generation (often utilizing pretrained language models) and\n148\tnon-autoregressive direct alignment. While autoregressive models possess strong language\n149\tpriors, they are prone to “hallucinations” when driven by noisy, low-signal-to-noise-ratio\n150\tEEG data, often generating fluent but semantically disconnected text.\n151\tTherefore,weestablisharobustConnectionistTemporalClassification(CTC)architecture\n152\tas our output decoder. Unlike autoregressive decoders, CTC strictly enforces monotonic\n153\ttemporal alignment between the brain signal and the character sequence. Given an encoder\n154\tf , the EEG signal is mapped to latent states H =f (X), followed by a linear prediction\n155\tθ θ\n156\thead g and optimized with the CTC negative log-likelihood:\n157\tctc\n158\t3\n159\t=== PAGE 4 ===\n160\tFigure 1: Overall architecture of EEGAlign. A shared sequence encoder with lightweight\n161\tsubject-specific adapters maps EEG to latent representations. A learned upsampler restores\n162\ttemporal density before applying three joint heads: CTC for decoding, text semantic\n163\talignment to BGE-M3 text embeddings, and audio acoustic alignment to wav2vec 2.0\n164\tfeatures.\n165\tL =−logP (y |g (H)). (1)\n166\tctc ctc ctc\n167\tAlthoughthisdecoderavoidsautoregressivehallucinationsanddirectlyoptimizescharacter-\n168\tlevel alignment, it relies almost entirely on the weak temporal correspondence between noisy\n169\tEEG frames and sparse text labels. Consequently, a pure CTC model often struggles to form\n170\tglobally discriminative representations necessary for accurate sentence-level identification.\n171\t3.2 The EEGAlign framework\n172\tTo overcome the limitations of the CTC-only decoder, we propose EEGAlign. A natural\n173\textension would be to introduce sentence-level semantic supervision by aligning EEG repre-\n174\tsentations with pretrained text embeddings. However, EEGAlign is built on the core view\n175\tthat semantic and acoustic supervision are complementary rather than interchangeable.\n176\tSentence-level text semantic alignment provides a stable global target for distinguishing\n177\tamongthousandsofChinesesentences,whereasaudioacousticalignmentsuppliestemporally\n178\tstructured information related to pronunciation, rhythm, and sub-second speech dynamics.\n179\tBecause the two supervision signals target different granularities of language representation,\n180\tthey are complementary rather than interchangeable. Accordingly, EEGAlign augments\n181\tthe CTC decoding path with two auxiliary alignment axes: semantic alignment to frozen\n182\tBGE-M3 text embeddings and acoustic alignment to wav2vec 2.0 speech features.\n183\tFigure1providesanoverview. Ratherthantreatingthemethodasarigidarchitecture,we\n184\torganizeitaroundonecorerequirement: theencodermustsupportthree levels of supervision\n185\tat once—shared subject-agnostic structure, subject-specific adaptation, and cross-modal\n186\talignment at both sentence and chunk levels.\n187\t3.3 Modeling with joint text-audio alignment\n188\tWe first describe EEGAlign at the level of its alignment and decoding objectives, treating\n189\tthe EEG encoder as an abstract module that produces a compact sequence representation\n190\tE ∈RB×Tp×d. This ordering emphasizes that the proposed text-audio alignment recipe is\n191\tnot tied to a particular backbone: any encoder that outputs a temporally ordered EEG\n192\trepresentation can be coupled with the same CTC, text-alignment, and audio-alignment\n193\theads.\n194\t4\n195\t=== PAGE 5 ===\n196\tAfter downsampling in the shared encoder, the sequence has only about 30 time steps,\n197\twhich is sufficient for global discrimination but too coarse for frame-aligned character\n198\tprediction and chunk-level acoustic matching. Rather than forcing CTC to operate directly\n199\ton this compressed representation, EEGAlign upsamples the EEG encoder output to 50\n200\tHz via a learned transposed convolution (kernel size and stride 5) followed by LayerNorm,\n201\tmatching the frame rate of wav2vec 2.0 audio features. This yields Eˆ ∈ RB×Tup×d with\n202\tT =T ×5≈150.\n203\tup p\n204\tFrom this upsampled sequence, EEGAlign derives three complementary output paths:\n205\tdirect decoding, semantic alignment, and acoustic alignment. The direct decoding path\n206\tlinearly projects Eˆ to frame-level logits P\n207\tctc\n208\t∈ RB×Tup×|V| and applies standard CTC\n209\tloss [25]. The semantic path mean-pools Eˆ into a sentence vector e¯ , projects it into\n210\teeg\n211\tBGE-M3 space [26], and aligns it to the frozen text embedding of the ground-truth sentence\n212\tvia InfoNCE [23] with temperature τ =0.05:\n213\texp (cid:0) sim(e¯ ,e¯+ )/τ (cid:1)\n214\tL =−log eeg text , (2)\n215\ttext (cid:80) exp (cid:0) sim(e¯ ,e¯j )/τ (cid:1)\n216\tj eeg text\n217\tThe acoustic path uses frozen wav2vec 2.0 features [27] as stable speech-side targets and\n218\tprojects the upsampled EEG sequence Eˆ into the same acoustic representation space. Both\n219\tEEG and wav2vec 2.0 audio features are average-pooled into non-overlapping windows using\n220\tchunk_size=20frames(400ms). Wethenperformchunk-levelInfoNCEcontrastivelearning\n221\tfor time alignment, where the EEG chunk and audio chunk at the same temporal position\n222\tform a positive pair, and all other chunks in the batch serve as negative samples.\n223\tCrucially, we employ a symmetric contrastive formulation to align noisy neural\n224\tsegments to stable speech features while keeping the speech-side representation fixed. We\n225\tdenote the directional chunk-level InfoNCE with query sequence x and key sequence y as\n226\tΦ(x,y)=− 1 (cid:88) log exp (cid:0) sim(x m ,y m +)/τ a (cid:1) . (3)\n227\tM (cid:80) exp (cid:0) sim(x ,y )/τ (cid:1)\n228\tm j m j a\n229\tThe bidirectional acoustic alignment loss is defined as Le2a = Φ(aˆ,sg(a)) and La2e =\n230\taudio audio\n231\tΦ(sg(a),aˆ), where aˆ and a denote the EEG and wav2vec 2.0 audio chunk projections at\n232\tm m\n233\tposition m, respectively, sg(·) indicates stop-gradient, and τ =0.07. Thus, gradients from\n234\ta\n235\tthe acoustic objective update the EEG-side representation, whereas the wav2vec 2.0 audio\n236\tfeatures remain fixed. The symmetric objective requires each EEG segment to identify its\n237\tcorresponding audio chunk and, conversely, to be uniquely discriminable when queried by\n238\tthe fixed audio anchor, thereby sharpening the representational structure against temporal\n239\tsmearing. The full objective is\n240\tL=λ L +λ L +λ (cid:0) Le2a +La2e (cid:1) , (4)\n241\tctc ctc text text audio audio audio\n242\twherethecontrastiveformulationexpressessupervisionintermsofrelativesimilarity. Thisis\n243\tpreferable to pointwise regression because EEG is noisy, temporally smeared, and amplitude-\n244\tvariable across subjects.\n245\tInference-time candidate evaluation. Following related works [20, 22], we evaluate\n246\ta closed-set sentence classification task with a fixed set of text candidates. At inference\n247\ttime, each candidate c is scored by fusing two complementary scores: (i) the semantic\n248\tscore, which computes the cosine similarity between the mean-pooled, ℓ -normalized EEG\n249\t2\n250\tprojection q and the pre-computed BGE-M3 text embedding etext; and (ii) the CTC\n251\teeg c\n252\tscore, which computes the log-likelihood logP (y |X) of the candidate token sequence\n253\tctc c\n254\tunder the CTC head. Prior to fusion, both scores are calibrated by candidate-specific\n255\tz-normalization using training-set statistics, denoted z (·). The final EEGAlign inference\n256\tc\n257\tscore is:\n258\tsDA(X,c)=αz (cid:0) cos(q ,etext) (cid:1) +(1−α)z (cid:0) logP (y |X) (cid:1) , (5)\n259\tc eeg c c ctc c\n260\t5\n261\t=== PAGE 6 ===\n262\twithα=0.4bydefault. ThecandidatewiththehighestsDA(X,c)isselectedasthepredicted\n263\tsentence.\n264\t3.4 Encoder backbone choices and subject adaptation\n265\tThis section specifies the encoder module that supplies the compact representation E\n266\tconsumed by the upsampler and the three supervision heads. EEGAlign is conceptualized\n267\tas a flexible framework rather than being tied to a specific network architecture. The raw\n268\tEEG signals are first mapped into temporal patches, and information is aggregated across\n269\tchannelsandtimetoproduceE ∈RB×Tp×d. Todemonstratethegeneralityofourtext-audio\n270\talignment recipe, we instantiate the encoder using two distinct backbones:\n271\t• Generic Encoder (Conformer): A lightweight sequence encoder trained entirely\n272\tfrom scratch. Each channel is processed independently by a strided 1D convolution\n273\t(kernel and stride equal to 25), yielding T = ⌊T/25⌋ patches of dimension d = 256.\n274\tp\n275\tFollowing learnable channel embeddings and Conditional Positional Encoding, the\n276\tsequence is processed by 3 Conformer [28] blocks. This 9.91M-parameter backbone\n277\testablishes that dual alignment works effectively even without large-scale pretraining.\n278\t• Foundation Encoder (LaBraM): To test whether the framework scales with pre-\n279\ttrained representations, we swap the generic encoder for LaBraM-base [14], a large-\n280\tscale EEG foundation model. LaBraM retains explicit per-channel spatial information\n281\tthrough its pretraining and maps the signals to a comparable latent space, requiring\n282\tno changes to our alignment heads.\n283\tA shared backbone may struggle to capture idiosyncratic subject patterns; however,\n284\tper-subject encoder replication lacks the necessary parameter efficiency. We therefore inject\n285\tsubjectadaptationonlyafterthefirsttwoencoderlayers, onceastablesharedrepresentation\n286\thas begun to form. For subject s at layer l, a bottleneck residual adapter [29] computes:\n287\t∆E(s) =SiLU (cid:0) E Wdown(cid:1) Wup, (6)\n288\tl l s s\n289\twhere W\n290\ts\n291\tdown ∈Rd×db , W\n292\ts\n293\tup ∈Rdb×d, and d\n294\tb\n295\t=32. We zero-initialize W\n296\ts\n297\tup so the adapter\n298\tstartsintheidentityregime. Theoverheadisnegligible(around16Kparameterspersubject).\n299\t3.5 Curriculum optimization\n300\tOptimizing Equation 4 from scratch is challenging. Early in training, CTC optimization\n301\tcan be unstable because the model has not yet learned reliable token alignments over a\n302\tlarge sequence space. Meanwhile, the contrastive objectives become informative only after\n303\tthe encoder has learned a minimally coherent representation space. We therefore train\n304\tEEGAlign via a three-stage curriculum. We first perform a semantic warm-starting stage\n305\tusing only L , allowing the encoder to establish a basic latent structure before character-\n306\ttext\n307\tlevel supervision is introduced. We then enter a bootstrapping stage that activates CTC\n308\tdecoding as the primary objective while keeping the two alignment branches as auxiliary\n309\tregularizers. Finally, we perform a joint refinement stage in which all three objectives remain\n310\tactive but the alignment terms are further down-weighted, so that CTC dominates the last\n311\tphase of optimization without losing the representational structure established earlier.\n312\t4 Experiments\n313\t4.1 Dataset\n314\tAll experiments use ChineseEEG-2 [19], which extends ChineseEEG [30] with active\n315\tproductionandpassiveperceptionofspeechoverthesameChineseliterarycorpus. Materials\n316\tconsist of the Chinese translation of The Little Prince (27 chapters, 1444 distinct Chinese\n317\t6\n318\t=== PAGE 7 ===\n319\tcharacters) and excerpts from Garnett Dream (8–9 recording sections, 2825 distinct Chinese\n320\tcharacters). Twelvehealthyright-handedparticipants(meanage21.9; 4male,8female)were\n321\trecruited. In the Reading Aloud (RA) condition, four participants read the texts aloud\n322\twhile 128-channel EEG and speech were recorded, yielding about 10.8 hours of paired EEG–\n323\tspeech data. In the Passive Listening (PL) condition, the remaining eight participants\n324\tlistened to the RA recordings, yielding about 21.6 hours of EEG. The RA and PL groups\n325\tare completely disjoint.\n326\tEEG was downsampled to 250 Hz, notch-filtered at 50 Hz, bandpass-filtered (1–40 Hz),\n327\tcleaned with FastICA, and common-average re-referenced, following the default processing\n328\tscripts. Were-segmentedtherawstreamstoobtainsentence-leveltexttargetsandchunk-level\n329\twav2vec 2.0 acoustic targets [27], yielding examples with 30≤T ≤750.\n330\tData Split Protocol. For the main comparisons, both RA and PL use a within-subject\n331\tstratified random split (90% train, 10% evaluation, seed = 42). All main-text accuracies are\n332\tcomputed on the held-out evaluation trials; Appendix E further reports a standard stratified\n333\tfive-fold cross-validation analysis on RA.\n334\t4.2 Evaluation protocol and metrics\n335\tWeevaluateallmethodsusingasampledclosed-setsentenceidentificationprotocol. Foreach\n336\tparadigm, the candidate pool C contains all unique sentences in the dataset, tokenized into\n337\tCTC targets y . For each evaluation EEG trial (ground-truth y∗), we construct a candidate\n338\ti\n339\tsubset C containing one positive and K (=100 by default) negative candidates sampled\n340\tsub\n341\tfrom C\\{y∗}.\n342\tWe report Top-1, Top-5, and Top-10 accuracies, Character Error Rate (CER), and\n343\tCRA@1 (Chance-Ratio Accuracy at 1), defined as Top-1 accuracy divided by the random-\n344\tchance baseline 1/(K+1); a CRA@1 of n× means the model is n times better than chance.\n345\t4.3 Experimental setup\n346\tAll models are trained on the same sentence-level splits and evaluated under the protocol\n347\tdescribed in Section 4.2. This ensures that performance differences reflect model design\n348\trather than data splits or candidate construction.\n349\tTable 5 in Appendix C.1 summarises the implementation of EEGAlign. Unless otherwise\n350\tstated, the encoder refers to the default lightweight instantiation described in Section 3;\n351\tthe alternative instantiation that replaces this encoder with a pretrained EEG foundation\n352\tencodersharesallothercomponentsverbatimandisusedasasecondreferenceconfiguration.\n353\t4.4 Baselines\n354\tWecompareEEGAlignagainstthreeexternalbaselineschosentoreflectdifferentstrategiesfor\n355\tstrengthening EEG decoding. LaBraM-MSE uses the pretrained LaBraM EEG foundation\n356\tmodel[14]toencodeEEGandtrainsaprojectionheadwithanMSEobjectivetomatchfrozen\n357\tBGE-M3 sentence embeddings. DeWave [4] is a representative EEG-to-text baseline based\n358\ton discrete neural tokenization and a pretrained language model; in our implementation, its\n359\tBART component is replaced with a Chinese-adapted BART checkpoint. SMM-Challenge\n360\tadaptstheEEG-Stimulus-Match-Mismatchmodel[22],whichcombinesself-supervisedspeech\n361\trepresentationsandcontextualtextembeddingsforstimulus-responsematching. Allbaselines\n362\tare evaluated under our closed-set protocol using their own available ranking scores.1\n363\t4.5 Main results\n364\tWe evaluate EEGAlign against three baselines using two distinct backbones: a lightweight\n365\tConformer and a pretrained LaBraM.\n366\t1Resources for external baselines: DeWave implementation, Chinese BART checkpoint, and SMM-\n367\tChallengecode.\n368\t7\n369\t=== PAGE 8 ===\n370\tTable 1: Closed-set sentence identification with one positive and 100 negatives.\n371\tRA(ReadingAloud) PL(PassiveListening)\n372\tMethod Encoder\n373\tTop-1 Top-5 Top-10 CRA@1 Top-1 Top-5 Top-10 CRA@1\n374\tLaBraM-MSE[14] LaBraM-base 23.33 45.58 56.92 23.57× 19.44 43.84 57.02 19.64×\n375\tDeWave[4] BART+VQ 1.73 6.86 15.19 1.75× 2.91 13.38 21.73 2.94×\n376\tSMM-Challenge[22] Brainnetwork 26.22 70.90 89.55 26.48× 3.11 9.57 17.00 3.14×\n377\tEEGAlign (ours) Conformer 69.62 91.15 94.04 70.31× 27.26 63.72 78.13 27.54×\n378\tEEGAlign (ours) LaBraM-base 82.37 91.60 94.29 83.20× 41.43 79.79 91.09 41.84×\n379\tAnalysis of Results. Table 1 presents the performance of EEGAlign. We highlight two\n380\tkey insights:\n381\t1)FrameworkEffectiveness: BothEEGAlignvariantsconsistentlyoutperformallbaselines\n382\tbyalargemargin. Thefoundation-encoderversionachieves82.37%Top-1onRAand41.43%\n383\ton PL, demonstrating that dense auxiliary alignment to both text and audio is critical for\n384\trobust decoding.\n385\t2) Encoder Robustness: Substantial gains are observed across both the lightweight Con-\n386\tformer and the pretrained LaBraM backbone, confirming the improvements are framework-\n387\tintrinsic. LaBraM’s extra capacity to preserve channel-level spatial information further\n388\tconditions the representation, yielding additional +12.8 and +14.2 point boosts on RA and\n389\tPL.\n390\tAppendix E further reports a standard stratified five-fold RA cross-validation analysis,\n391\twith a mean Top-1 accuracy of 82.20% and a standard deviation of 1.68 percentage points.\n392\t4.6 Ablation studies\n393\tBuilding on the main results, we conduct ablation studies to isolate the contributions of\n394\tEEGAlign’s individual components. All ablations use the 9.91M-parameter Conformer\n395\tbackbone and the same closed-set protocol with K = 100 candidates as in Table 1. The\n396\tvariantsinTable2followthetablenotation: w/o audioremovesaudioalignment, w/o text\n397\tremovestextalignment,w/o bothremovesbothalignmentobjectives,w/o adapterremoves\n398\tsubject-specific adapters, and w/o curriculum removes the staged training curriculum.\n399\tTable 2: Alignment-component ablation on the Conformer encoder.\n400\tRA (Reading Aloud) PL (Passive Listening)\n401\tVariant\n402\tTop-1 Top-5 Top-10 CER (%)↓ Top-1 Top-5 Top-10 CER (%)↓\n403\tFull EEGAlign (text + audio) 69.62 91.15 94.04 28.90 27.26 63.72 78.13 68.30\n404\tw/o audio 56.92 79.55 82.05 59.80 21.12 58.62 74.71 71.00\n405\tw/o text 62.63 80.71 84.49 37.60 26.92 62.47 73.88 64.70\n406\tw/o both 66.28 85.51 88.59 34.30 23.55 59.92 70.60 67.10\n407\tw/o adapter 63.59 88.65 92.31 34.55 25.60 61.51 71.67 67.07\n408\tw/o curriculum 67.05 89.74 93.91 31.30 25.54 60.47 72.08 67.67\n409\tOverall, Table 2 shows that EEGAlign’s gains come from three interacting factors: joint\n410\ttext–audio alignment, subject-specific adaptation, and progressive curriculum optimization.\n411\t(i) The alignment objectives are complementary rather than independently additive. On\n412\tRA, adding only one auxiliary axis is worse than using no auxiliary alignment: text-only\n413\talignment drops to 56.92 and audio-only alignment reaches 62.63, both below the CTC-only\n414\tbaseline (66.28). In contrast, combining the two axes improves Top-1 to 69.62, the best\n415\tresult among all variants. This non-monotonic pattern suggests that either text or audio\n416\tsupervisionalonemayimposeanincompleteconstraintontheEEGencoder,whiletheirjoint\n417\tuse regularizes the representation from complementary perspectives. (ii) The subject-specific\n418\tadapters are also important: removing them reduces Top-1 from 69.62 to 63.59 on RA\n419\tand from 27.26 to 25.60 on PL. This indicates that the lightweight adapters help absorb\n420\t8\n421\t=== PAGE 9 ===\n422\tinter-subject variability without replacing the shared encoder. (iii) Finally, removing the\n423\tcurriculum degrades both paradigms, indicating that progressive optimization is necessary\n424\tto stabilize the heterogeneous CTC, text, and audio objectives.\n425\t4.7 Analysis\n426\tThis section provides a detailed analysis of the inference-time fusion gain, candidate-set\n427\tsensitivity, andaudio-alignmentweightsensitivity. Wefurtherexaminewhetherthesemantic\n428\tbranch reshapes the encoder representation itself, with complementary paradigm-specific\n429\tanalyses deferred to Appendix G.\n430\tFusion gain over CTC-only ranking. We compare EEGAlign’s default fused score\n431\tsDA (Eq. 5, α=0.4) to the degenerate case of ranking candidates by the CTC log-likelihood\n432\tonly, using the same trained model. Figure 2(a) shows the absolute Top-1 gain of fusion\n433\tover CTC-only graphically (full numerical details are provided in Appendix B).\n434\t80\n435\t60\n436\t40\n437\t20\n438\t0\n439\tRA RA PL PL\n440\tConformer LaBraM Conformer LaBraM\n441\t)%(\n442\tycarucca\n443\t1-poT\n444\t(a) Fusion gain over CTC-only\n445\t100\n446\tF\n447\tC\n448\tu\n449\tT\n450\ts\n451\tC\n452\te\n453\t-\n454\td\n455\to n\n456\t(\n457\tly\n458\t=0+.44).4\n459\t+3.4 80\n460\t60 +11.5\n461\t40\n462\t+1.6\n463\t20\n464\t0\n465\t5 20 50 100 500\n466\tCandidate-set size K\n467\t)%(\n468\tycarucca\n469\t1-poT\n470\t(b) Candidate-set sensitivity (LaBraM)\n471\t85\n472\t84\n473\t83\n474\t82\n475\t81\n476\t80 RA Top-1 P R L A T C o R p A -1 @1 79\n477\tPL CRA@1\n478\t78\n479\ta=0.5 a=0.1\n480\t)%(\n481\tycarucca\n482\t1-poT\n483\t(c) Audio-weight sensitivity (RA, LaBraM)\n484\t400\n485\tTop-1 (%)\n486\t350 CER (%)\n487\t300\n488\t250 82.37 81.67 200\n489\t150\n490\t100 50\n491\t0\n492\t)×( 1@ARC\n493\t20\n494\t19\n495\t18\n496\t17\n497\t16\n498\t15\n499\t)%( REC 17.82 17.26\n500\tFigure 2: Analysis of fusion gain, hyperparameter sensitivity, and qualitative case studies.\n501\t(a) Absolute Top-1 gain of fused score (α = 0.4) over CTC-only scoring; C and L denote\n502\tConformer and LaBraM. (b) Candidate-set sensitivity for EEGAlign-LaBraM with varying\n503\tnegativesizesK. (c)Sensitivitytotheaudio-alignmentweightλ onRA.(d)Qualitative\n504\taudio\n505\tUMAPvisualisation: EEGAlign(reddots)movesrepresentationsofCTC-onlybaseline(blue\n506\tdots) toward ground-truth BGE-M3 embeddings (stars).\n507\tTwo points are worth emphasising. (i) The fused score dominates the CTC-only score\n508\tin every setting, confirming that the semantic embedding head and the CTC head provide\n509\tcomplementary evidence at inference time. (ii) The magnitude of the gain depends strongly\n510\ton paradigm and encoder: the largest gain (+11.54) is on PL with the LaBraM encoder,\n511\twhere CTC evidence is weak and the frozen BGE-M3 anchor is useful as a corrective signal\n512\t(Table 4); the smallest gains (+1.5 to +4.4) are on RA and on the Conformer variants,\n513\twhere the CTC branch is already near-saturated. This is consistent with the view that\n514\ttext alignment functions mainly as a complementary inference-time score, rather than as a\n515\treplacement for the CTC score.\n516\tCandidate-set sensitivity. BecausetheabsoluteTop-1accuracyofclosed-setidentifica-\n517\ttion depends mechanically on the candidate-pool size K+1, we evaluate EEGAlign-LaBraM\n518\ton both RA and PL under five candidate sizes (K ∈{5,20,50,100,500}). As Figure 2(b)\n519\tsummarises, RA Top-1 degrades gracefully from 93.21% at K=5 to 67.50% at K=500, while\n520\tPL Top-1 drops from 86.32% to 16.38%. Crucially, CRA@1 over chance grows with K\n521\tfor both paradigms. Although direct comparisons warrant caution due to differences in\n522\tlanguageandstimuli,ourPLresultsgenerallyalignwith—and,underourclosed-setsentence-\n523\tidentification protocol, outperform—representative auditory speech-perception decoding and\n524\tICASSP challenge-style match-mismatch systems [20, 22]. Full per-K results are reported in\n525\tAppendix C.2.\n526\tAudio-weight sensitivity. We also examine the model’s sensitivity to the audio-\n527\talignment weight λ on RA. As shown in Figure 2(c), the default gentler audio-alignment\n528\taudio\n529\tweight (λ =0.1) yields slightly better Top-1 accuracy (82.37% vs. 81.67%) and lower\n530\taudio\n531\tCharacter Error Rate (17.26% vs. 17.82%) compared to the stronger setting λ =0.5,\n532\taudio\n533\t9\n534\t=== PAGE 10 ===\n535\tindicating that while acoustic supervision is crucial, it should not overpower the primary\n536\tCTC objective.\n537\tRepresentation-level structural alignment. The fusion analysis above demonstrates\n538\tthat the semantic branch contributes useful inference-time evidence. We compute Centered\n539\tKernelAlignment(CKA)[31]betweentheupsampledEEGencoderoutput(d=256,afterthe\n540\tupsampler, before any projection head) and frozen BGE-M3 text embeddings (d=1024) on\n541\t200 held-out RA evaluation trials. The aggregate CKA results are reported in Appendix H;\n542\tin brief, EEGAlign achieves the highest structural alignment, increasing linear CKA from\n543\t0.294 to 0.354 and RBF CKA from 0.332 to 0.402 relative to the CTC-only baseline, while\n544\teach single-axis variant improves CKA only mildly. This supports the same conclusion as\n545\tTable 2.\n546\tFigure 2(d) gives a compact qualitative view of this effect on representative rescued cases.\n547\tTheUMAPprojectionshowsthatEEGAlignmovestheCTC-onlybaselinetowardthecorrect\n548\tBGE-M3 sentence anchor; the retained CKA summary and encoder-to-text representation\n549\tmeasurements are provided in Appendices H and G.\n550\tCase-level retrieval comparison. Beyondtheaggregatemetrics, weinspectindividual\n551\tretrieval outcomes by examining the displayed case examples produced by EEGAlign, the\n552\tCTC-only scoring baseline, and LaBraM-MSE (embedding-only retrieval without CTC). On\n553\t10representativetrials(5RA,5PL)wherebothbaselinesfailatTop-1butEEGAlignsucceeds,\n554\tthe CTC-only baseline ranks the ground-truth sentence at positions 20–92 (median ∼82)\n555\tand LaBraM-MSE at positions 2–93, while EEGAlign places the correct sentence at rank 1\n556\tin every case. This indicates that text-audio alignment leverages complementary evidence:\n557\tthe alignment score and CTC log-likelihood provide strong sentence-level corrections to\n558\teach other. Five cases for each paradigm and per-method rank comparisons are provided in\n559\tAppendix I.1.\n560\t5 Conclusion and limitations\n561\tEEGAligninvestigateswhethertextandaudioalignmentprovidecomplementarysupervision\n562\tfor Chinese EEG-to-text decoding in speech production and perception. Our results suggest\n563\tthatsimultaneousEEG–textandEEG–speechalignmentyieldsstrongclosed-setclassification\n564\tperformance under a 1-in-101 protocol and, crucially, does so across two distinct encoder\n565\tinstantiations. The generic Conformer and the pretrained LaBraM achieve up to 69.62% and\n566\t82.37% Top-1 on RA, respectively, outperforming all external baselines by large margins.\n567\tThis consistent gain confirms the robustness of the text-audio alignment recipe, independent\n568\tof the specific backbone.\n569\tThe two alignment supervision signals are not interchangeable: audio alignment acts as\n570\tthe dominant training-time auxiliary, while the text alignment head contributes primarily as\n571\ta complementary inference-time score, with the largest fusion gain (+11.5 Top-1) on PL,\n572\twhere the sequence-level signal is weak. A gentle audio-alignment weight (λ = 0.1)\n573\taudio\n574\tis sufficient and slightly preferable to a stronger one. Per-subject analysis (Appendix D)\n575\tshows that all 12 subjects with reported values benefit substantially from full EEGAlign\n576\tover CTC-only, with a tight spread across subjects (Top-1 std. ≤ 2.3 points on RA and\n577\t≤1.7 points on PL for the best foundation-encoder variant). Together with the stratified\n578\tfive-foldRAcross-validationresult(Top-182.20±1.68%;AppendixE),thesefindingsindicate\n579\tthat the proposed alignment recipe is stable under the present closed-set, subject-aware\n580\tevaluation. This evaluation setting, however, is still a constrained step toward practical\n581\tEEG-to-text communication: the current decoder scores a fixed candidate set rather than\n582\tperforming open-ended beam-search sentence decoding, and the subject-specific adapters\n583\trequire observed subject data rather than supporting zero-shot decoding for unseen users.\n584\tMore broadly, the modest number of subjects and limited amount of data also constrain the\n585\tintegration of EEG encoders with larger generative language decoders. Addressing these\n586\tlimitations is a key step toward deployable non-invasive Chinese neural communication\n587\tsystems.\n588\t10\n589\t=== PAGE 11 ===\n590\tReferences\n591\t[1] Francis R Willett, Erin M Kunz, Chaofei Fan, Donald T Avansino, Guy H Wilson, Eun Young\n592\tChoi, Foram Kamdar, Matthew F Glasser, Leigh R Hochberg, Shaul Druckmann, et al. A\n593\thigh-performance speech neuroprosthesis. Nature, 620(7976):1031–1036, 2023.\n594\t[2] Vinay Jayaram, Morteza Alamgir, Yasemin Altun, Bernhard Scholkopf, and Moritz Grosse-\n595\tWentrup. Transfer learning in brain-computer interfaces. IEEE Computational Intelligence\n596\tMagazine, 11(1):20–31, 2016.\n597\t[3] Bo-Qun Ma, He Li, Wei-Long Zheng, and Bao-Liang Lu. Reducing the subject variability of\n598\teeg signals with adversarial domain generalization. In International Conference on Neural\n599\tInformation Processing, pages 30–42. Springer, 2019.\n600\t[4] Yiqun Duan, Jinzhao Zhou, Zhen Wang, Yu-Kai Wang, and Chin-teng Lin. Dewave: Discrete\n601\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "e3cc4b93-fc68-46ce-b710-420272e6b32a", "uuid": "b18b98db-dcc6-4ecf-a7c0-8a89cc40c91e", "timestamp": "2026-07-29T08:21:45.761Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/e3cc4b93-fc68-46ce-b710-420272e6b32a/tool-results/bvq0z51es.txt", "content": "PAGES=21\n=== PAGE 1 ===\nJoint Text-Audio Alignment for EEG-to-Text Decoding\nin Chinese Speech Production and Perception\nTian Zheng*1,2, Xurong Xie2, Xinxin Zhu3, Xiaolan Peng2, and Feng Tian2\n1University of the Chinese Academy of Sciences\n2Institute of Software, Chinese Academy of Sciences\n3Beijing Language and Culture University\n*E-mail: zhengtian24@mails.ucas.ac.cn\nJuly 29, 2026\nAbstract\nDecodingspeechinformationdirectlyfromscalpelectroencephalography(EEG)into\ntext provides a potential non-invasive neural communication pathway for individuals\nwith severe speech and motor impairments. Compared with invasive approaches such\nas electrocorticography, EEG is safer and more widely deployable, but it remains\nsubstantially more challenging to decode. This challenge is exacerbated in Chinese\nsentence decoding, which must handle a high-dimensional output space with thousands\nof characters, severe inter-subject variability, and low signal-to-noise ratios for text\nalignment. Existingmethodscommittoasinglesupervisoryaxis—eithertextsemantics\nor audio acoustic features—yet neither can simultaneously satisfy the demands of\nsentence-level discriminability and fine-grained temporal resolution required for large-\nvocabulary Chinese decoding. We introduce EEGAlign, a novel parameter-efficient\nframework that jointly aligns EEG along two axes: text alignment with BGE-M3 text\nembeddings and audio alignment with wav2vec 2.0 speech features via contrastive\nlearning, followed by CTC character-sequence decoding. On ChineseEEG-2 data,\nEEGAlignyieldsstate-of-the-artclosed-setsentenceclassificationperformance,reaching\nup to 82.37% Top-1 accuracy on Reading Aloud EEG and 41.43% on Passive Listening\nEEGoutof101candidates.Ablationstudiesshowthatthetwoalignmentaxesarehighly\ncomplementary: combining them yields consistently better performance than either\nalone. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study to decode large-vocabulary\nChinesesentencesfromnon-invasiveEEGduringovertspeechproductionwhileachieving\nstrong classification performance in a relatively large closed-set candidate-sentence\nsetting.\n1 Introduction\nFor individuals whose speech and motor functions are severely impaired by conditions\nsuch as locked-in syndrome, ALS, or stroke, brain–computer interfaces (BCIs) that decode\nspeech intentions from neural activity offer a vital route toward restoring communication.\nWhile invasive recordings have demonstrated high-performance decoding [1], the necessity of\nsurgical implantation limits their broad clinical deployment. Scalp electroencephalography\n(EEG) presents a non-invasive, accessible, and safe alternative, yet it must overcome a\nsignificant “quality gap” characterized by low spatial resolution and three primary technical\nhurdles. First, signal weakness requires extracting speech and language activity from noisy\nrecordings affected by artifacts and background neural noise. Second, subject variability\nin cortical anatomy and neurophysiology creates distribution shifts that hinder model\n1\n6202\nluJ\n82\n]IA.sc[\n1v62652.7062:viXra\n=== PAGE 2 ===\ngeneralization [2, 3]. Finally, output-space complexity makes mapping neural representations\nto discrete tokens particularly difficult. These issues are further exacerbated in Chinese\nsentence-level decoding, making it a major challenge for translational neural decoding.\nRecent progress in EEG-to-text has addressed these challenges along three empirical\nlines, though most advances target passive listening or silent reading and evaluate open-\nended generation metrics (BLEU/ROUGE) rather than exact-match accuracy. The first\nline integrates pretrained language models [4–6] or scales to LLM backbones with semantic\nreranking [7–9]. While these methods gain sentence-level discriminability from powerful\ntext priors, they share a critical limitation: alignment is purely semantic, providing no fine-\ngrained temporal supervision—and their autoregressive decoders are prone to hallucination\nwhen EEG input is noisy. The second line reconstructs acoustic intermediates such as\nmel-spectrograms [10–12], motivated by the observation that direct text discretization\ndiscards prosodic structure still reflected in neural activity. These systems capture temporal\ndynamicsbuttreatacousticreconstructionasthemaingoalandrarelyconsidersentence-level\ndiscriminability. The third line builds foundation-scale EEG encoders via self-supervised\npretraining [13, 14], offering generic representations that still require task-specific alignment\nfor downstream decoding. Crucially, all three paradigms have been driven primarily by\nnon-Chinese datasets (e.g., ZuCo [15]), and none jointly exploits text and audio supervision.\nThe few studies investigating overt speech production typically restrict decoding to\nisolated syllables or words [16, 17]. EEG-to-text decoding remains largely unexplored for\ncontinuous active speech; we found only one preprint [18] using 175 hours of data and\nreporting relatively weak classification. In this setting, both articulation-induced motor\nsignals and linguistic representations are crucial for decoding. ChineseEEG-2 [19] provides\nthe first Chinese continuous speech EEG dataset with matched Reading Aloud (RA) and\nPassive Listening (PL) paradigms. Yet no existing method jointly exploits text and audio\nsupervisiononthisdata: textsemanticalignmentalonelacksfine-grainedtemporalresolution,\nwhileaudioacousticalignmentaloneoffersweakinter-sentenceseparability. Combiningthem\naddresses complementary granularities of speech and language representation.\nBuildingonthisobservation,weproposeEEGAlign,aparameter-efficientframeworkthat\njointly optimizes an EEG encoder with text alignment (to frozen BGE-M3 text embeddings)\nand audio alignment (to wav2vec 2.0 speech features) via contrastive learning alongside CTC\ncharacter-sequence decoding. Lightweight bottleneck adapters (∼16K params per subject)\nhandle inter-subject variability, and a three-stage curriculum stabilizes multi-objective\noptimization. Experiments conducted on ChineseEEG-2 demonstrate the effectiveness of\nEEGAlign. The framework is backbone-agnostic: both a 9.91M-parameter Conformer and\na pretrained LaBraM encoder [14] achieve strong performance, confirming that gains stem\nfrom the alignment formulation. Our contributions are: (i) we propose EEGAlign as a\nprincipled combination of complementary text-semantic and speech-acoustic supervision\nfor EEG-to-text decoding in speech production and perception; (ii) EEGAlign achieves\nstate-of-the-art closed-set sentence classification performance on ChineseEEG-2, reaching up\nto 82.37% on RA and 41.43% on PL (101 candidates); and (iii) we systematically analyze\nthe alignment mechanisms through ablations, representational geometry, and candidate-set\nsensitivity. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first study to decode large-vocabulary\nChinese sentences from non-invasive EEG during overt speech production while achieving\nstrong classification performance in a relatively large closed-set candidate-sentence setting.\n2 Related work\nNon-invasive EEG decoding has transitioned from rudimentary discrete unit classification\ntowardcontinuouslanguagereconstructionandretrieval. Currentfrontiersinthefieldfocuson\ndecoding continuous language directly from neural signals, with technical evolution primarily\nbifurcating into two trajectories: acoustic reconstruction and cross-modal alignment.\nEarly investigations established the feasibility of linking non-invasive brain recordings\n2\n=== PAGE 3 ===\nto continuous speech via contrastive learning between speech representations and neural\nactivity [20]. Subsequent research has focused on the fine-grained reconstruction of acoustic\nfeatures, such as mel-spectrograms, by refining sequence modeling through State Space\nModels (SSMs) and dynamic multiscale fusion [10, 12, 11]. However, these methods center\non acoustic fidelity, which introduces an objective mismatch with the ultimate goal of text\nidentification. Furthermore, they often lack explicit sentence-level semantic alignment. Con-\nversely, another research path aims to establish direct associations between EEG signals and\ntext or semantic representations. This is achieved through multi-task contrastive learning [7],\nCLIP-style architectures [21], or quantization-based discretization for language model inte-\ngration [4]. Notably, Wang et al. [22] demonstrated in a match-mismatch classification task\nthat the simultaneous utilization of self-supervised speech representations and contextual\ntext embeddings effectively enhances performance. This provides preliminary evidence for\nthe complementary value of multimodal (acoustic and semantic) features. Nevertheless, a\nfundamental disparity exists between classification tasks and continuous sentence decoding:\nwhile the former merely identifies correspondences between signals, the latter requires the\nreconstruction of precise, coherent linguistic sequences from inherently noisy and ambiguous\nneural activity.\nTheaforementionedprogresshasbeenpredominantlydrivenbyEnglishbenchmarks. The\ntonalnatureofMandarinandthescarcityofcontinuousspoken-EEGcorporaposesignificantly\ngreater challenges for sentence-level decoding. Current non-invasive Chinese spoken-EEG\nresearch remains confined to sub-word units, such as monosyllabic classification [16] or\npinyin-level production [17]. Consequently, decoding large-vocabulary continuous Chinese\nsentences remains a research gap.\nRecent advancements in representation learning and subject adaptation provide the\ntechnical bedrock for addressing these complexities. Large-scale EEG foundation models,\nsuch as BIOT [13] and LaBraM [14], have demonstrated that self-supervised pretraining can\nyield robust, transferable neural representations. For cross-modal bridging, InfoNCE-style\ncontrastive learning has emerged as the dominant approach [23]. To mitigate severe inter-\nsubject variability, Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) techniques, such as LoRA [24],\nhave been widely adopted.\n3 Method\n3.1 Problem formulation and CTC-based decoder\nGiven a multichannel EEG recording X ∈ RC×T acquired at 250 Hz from C = 128 scalp\nelectrodesoveratemporalwindowoflengthT ≤750,ourgoalistodecodethecorresponding\nChinese character sequence y =(y ,y ,...,y ), where each y is one Chinese character and\n1 2 L i\nL is the number of characters in the target sentence. Here T denotes the number of EEG\ntime samples, whereas L denotes the output length; the two are not assumed to match, and\ntheir alignment is handled by CTC. This setting is highly challenging due to the extremely\nlarge output space, severe inter-subject variability, and the intrinsically weak alignment\nbetween scalp EEG and discrete text tokens.\nCurrent approaches to EEG-to-text decoding generally fall into two paradigms: autore-\ngressive sequence-to-sequence generation (often utilizing pretrained language models) and\nnon-autoregressive direct alignment. While autoregressive models possess strong language\npriors, they are prone to “hallucinations” when driven by noisy, low-signal-to-noise-ratio\nEEG data, often generating fluent but semantically disconnected text.\nTherefore,weestablisharobustConnectionistTemporalClassification(CTC)architecture\nas our output decoder. Unlike autoregressive decoders, CTC strictly enforces monotonic\ntemporal alignment between the brain signal and the character sequence. Given an encoder\nf , the EEG signal is mapped to latent states H =f (X), followed by a linear prediction\nθ θ\nhead g and optimized with the CTC negative log-likelihood:\nctc\n3\n=== PAGE 4 ===\nFigure 1: Overall architecture of EEGAlign. A shared sequence encoder with lightweight\nsubject-specific adapters maps EEG to latent representations. A learned upsampler restores\ntemporal density before applying three joint heads: CTC for decoding, text semantic\nalignment to BGE-M3 text embeddings, and audio acoustic alignment to wav2vec 2.0\nfeatures.\nL =−logP (y |g (H)). (1)\nctc ctc ctc\nAlthoughthisdecoderavoidsautoregressivehallucinationsanddirectlyoptimizescharacter-\nlevel alignment, it relies almost entirely on the weak temporal correspondence between noisy\nEEG frames and sparse text labels. Consequently, a pure CTC model often struggles to form\nglobally discriminative representations necessary for accurate sentence-level identification.\n3.2 The EEGAlign framework\nTo overcome the limitations of the CTC-only decoder, we propose EEGAlign. A natural\nextension would be to introduce sentence-level semantic supervision by aligning EEG repre-\nsentations with pretrained text embeddings. However, EEGAlign is built on the core view\nthat semantic and acoustic supervision are complementary rather than interchangeable.\nSentence-level text semantic alignment provides a stable global target for distinguishing\namongthousandsofChinesesentences,whereasaudioacousticalignmentsuppliestemporally\nstructured information related to pronunciation, rhythm, and sub-second speech dynamics.\nBecause the two supervision signals target different granularities of language representation,\nthey are complementary rather than interchangeable. Accordingly, EEGAlign augments\nthe CTC decoding path with two auxiliary alignment axes: semantic alignment to frozen\nBGE-M3 text embeddings and acoustic alignment to wav2vec 2.0 speech features.\nFigure1providesanoverview. Ratherthantreatingthemethodasarigidarchitecture,we\norganizeitaroundonecorerequirement: theencodermustsupportthree levels of supervision\nat once—shared subject-agnostic structure, subject-specific adaptation, and cross-modal\nalignment at both sentence and chunk levels.\n3.3 Modeling with joint text-audio alignment\nWe first describe EEGAlign at the level of its alignment and decoding objectives, treating\nthe EEG encoder as an abstract module that produces a compact sequence representation\nE ∈RB×Tp×d. This ordering emphasizes that the proposed text-audio alignment recipe is\nnot tied to a particular backbone: any encoder that outputs a temporally ordered EEG\nrepresentation can be coupled with the same CTC, text-alignment, and audio-alignment\nheads.\n4\n=== PAGE 5 ===\nAfter downsampling in the shared encoder, the sequence has only about 30 time steps,\nwhich is sufficient for global discrimination but too coarse for frame-aligned character\nprediction and chunk-level acoustic matching. Rather than forcing CTC to operate directly\non this compressed representation, EEGAlign upsamples the EEG encoder output to 50\nHz via a learned transposed convolution (kernel size and stride 5) followed by LayerNorm,\nmatching the frame rate of wav2vec 2.0 audio features. This yields Eˆ ∈ RB×Tup×d with\nT =T ×5≈150.\nup p\nFrom this upsampled sequence, EEGAlign derives three complementary output paths:\ndirect decoding, semantic alignment, and acoustic alignment. The direct decoding path\nlinearly projects Eˆ to frame-level logits P\nctc\n∈ RB×Tup×|V| and applies standard CTC\nloss [25]. The semantic path mean-pools Eˆ into a sentence vector e¯ , projects it into\neeg\nBGE-M3 space [26], and aligns it to the frozen text embedding of the ground-truth sentence\nvia InfoNCE [23] with temperature τ =0.05:\nexp (cid:0) sim(e¯ ,e¯+ )/τ (cid:1)\nL =−log eeg text , (2)\ntext (cid:80) exp (cid:0) sim(e¯ ,e¯j )/τ (cid:1)\nj eeg text\nThe acoustic path uses frozen wav2vec 2.0 features [27] as stable speech-side targets and\nprojects the upsampled EEG sequence Eˆ into the same acoustic representation space. Both\nEEG and wav2vec 2.0 audio features are average-pooled into non-overlapping windows using\nchunk_size=20frames(400ms). Wethenperformchunk-levelInfoNCEcontrastivelearning\nfor time alignment, where the EEG chunk and audio chunk at the same temporal position\nform a positive pair, and all other chunks in the batch serve as negative samples.\nCrucially, we employ a symmetric contrastive formulation to align noisy neural\nsegments to stable speech features while keeping the speech-side representation fixed. We\ndenote the directional chunk-level InfoNCE with query sequence x and key sequence y as\nΦ(x,y)=− 1 (cid:88) log exp (cid:0) sim(x m ,y m +)/τ a (cid:1) . (3)\nM (cid:80) exp (cid:0) sim(x ,y )/τ (cid:1)\nm j m j a\nThe bidirectional acoustic alignment loss is defined as Le2a = Φ(aˆ,sg(a)) and La2e =\naudio audio\nΦ(sg(a),aˆ), where aˆ and a denote the EEG and wav2vec 2.0 audio chunk projections at\nm m\nposition m, respectively, sg(·) indicates stop-gradient, and τ =0.07. Thus, gradients from\na\nthe acoustic objective update the EEG-side representation, whereas the wav2vec 2.0 audio\nfeatures remain fixed. The symmetric objective requires each EEG segment to identify its\ncorresponding audio chunk and, conversely, to be uniquely discriminable when queried by\nthe fixed audio anchor, thereby sharpening the representational structure against temporal\nsmearing. The full objective is\nL=λ L +λ L +λ (cid:0) Le2a +La2e (cid:1) , (4)\nctc ctc text text audio audio audio\nwherethecontrastiveformulationexpressessupervisionintermsofrelativesimilarity. Thisis\npreferable to pointwise regression because EEG is noisy, temporally smeared, and amplitude-\nvariable across subjects.\nInference-time candidate evaluation. Following related works [20, 22], we evaluate\na closed-set sentence classification task with a fixed set of text candidates. At inference\ntime, each candidate c is scored by fusing two complementary scores: (i) the semantic\nscore, which computes the cosine similarity between the mean-pooled, ℓ -normalized EEG\n2\nprojection q and the pre-computed BGE-M3 text embedding etext; and (ii) the CTC\neeg c\nscore, which computes the log-likelihood logP (y |X) of the candidate token sequence\nctc c\nunder the CTC head. Prior to fusion, both scores are calibrated by candidate-specific\nz-normalization using training-set statistics, denoted z (·). The final EEGAlign inference\nc\nscore is:\nsDA(X,c)=αz (cid:0) cos(q ,etext) (cid:1) +(1−α)z (cid:0) logP (y |X) (cid:1) , (5)\nc eeg c c ctc c\n5\n=== PAGE 6 ===\nwithα=0.4bydefault. ThecandidatewiththehighestsDA(X,c)isselectedasthepredicted\nsentence.\n3.4 Encoder backbone choices and subject adaptation\nThis section specifies the encoder module that supplies the compact representation E\nconsumed by the upsampler and the three supervision heads. EEGAlign is conceptualized\nas a flexible framework rather than being tied to a specific network architecture. The raw\nEEG signals are first mapped into temporal patches, and information is aggregated across\nchannelsandtimetoproduceE ∈RB×Tp×d. Todemonstratethegeneralityofourtext-audio\nalignment recipe, we instantiate the encoder using two distinct backbones:\n• Generic Encoder (Conformer): A lightweight sequence encoder trained entirely\nfrom scratch. Each channel is processed independently by a strided 1D convolution\n(kernel and stride equal to 25), yielding T = ⌊T/25⌋ patches of dimension d = 256.\np\nFollowing learnable channel embeddings and Conditional Positional Encoding, the\nsequence is processed by 3 Conformer [28] blocks. This 9.91M-parameter backbone\nestablishes that dual alignment works effectively even without large-scale pretraining.\n• Foundation Encoder (LaBraM): To test whether the framework scales with pre-\ntrained representations, we swap the generic encoder for LaBraM-base [14], a large-\nscale EEG foundation model. LaBraM retains explicit per-channel spatial information\nthrough its pretraining and maps the signals to a comparable latent space, requiring\nno changes to our alignment heads.\nA shared backbone may struggle to capture idiosyncratic subject patterns; however,\nper-subject encoder replication lacks the necessary parameter efficiency. We therefore inject\nsubjectadaptationonlyafterthefirsttwoencoderlayers, onceastablesharedrepresentation\nhas begun to form. For subject s at layer l, a bottleneck residual adapter [29] computes:\n∆E(s) =SiLU (cid:0) E Wdown(cid:1) Wup, (6)\nl l s s\nwhere W\ns\ndown ∈Rd×db , W\ns\nup ∈Rdb×d, and d\nb\n=32. We zero-initialize W\ns\nup so the adapter\nstartsintheidentityregime. Theoverheadisnegligible(around16Kparameterspersubject).\n3.5 Curriculum optimization\nOptimizing Equation 4 from scratch is challenging. Early in training, CTC optimization\ncan be unstable because the model has not yet learned reliable token alignments over a\nlarge sequence space. Meanwhile, the contrastive objectives become informative only after\nthe encoder has learned a minimally coherent representation space. We therefore train\nEEGAlign via a three-stage curriculum. We first perform a semantic warm-starting stage\nusing only L , allowing the encoder to establish a basic latent structure before character-\ntext\nlevel supervision is introduced. We then enter a bootstrapping stage that activates CTC\ndecoding as the primary objective while keeping the two alignment branches as auxiliary\nregularizers. Finally, we perform a joint refinement stage in which all three objectives remain\nactive but the alignment terms are further down-weighted, so that CTC dominates the last\nphase of optimization without losing the representational structure established earlier.\n4 Experiments\n4.1 Dataset\nAll experiments use ChineseEEG-2 [19], which extends ChineseEEG [30] with active\nproductionandpassiveperceptionofspeechoverthesameChineseliterarycorpus. Materials\nconsist of the Chinese translation of The Little Prince (27 chapters, 1444 distinct Chinese\n6\n=== PAGE 7 ===\ncharacters) and excerpts from Garnett Dream (8–9 recording sections, 2825 distinct Chinese\ncharacters). Twelvehealthyright-handedparticipants(meanage21.9; 4male,8female)were\nrecruited. In the Reading Aloud (RA) condition, four participants read the texts aloud\nwhile 128-channel EEG and speech were recorded, yielding about 10.8 hours of paired EEG–\nspeech data. In the Passive Listening (PL) condition, the remaining eight participants\nlistened to the RA recordings, yielding about 21.6 hours of EEG. The RA and PL groups\nare completely disjoint.\nEEG was downsampled to 250 Hz, notch-filtered at 50 Hz, bandpass-filtered (1–40 Hz),\ncleaned with FastICA, and common-average re-referenced, following the default processing\nscripts. Were-segmentedtherawstreamstoobtainsentence-leveltexttargetsandchunk-level\nwav2vec 2.0 acoustic targets [27], yielding examples with 30≤T ≤750.\nData Split Protocol. For the main comparisons, both RA and PL use a within-subject\nstratified random split (90% train, 10% evaluation, seed = 42). All main-text accuracies are\ncomputed on the held-out evaluation trials; Appendix E further reports a standard stratified\nfive-fold cross-validation analysis on RA.\n4.2 Evaluation protocol and metrics\nWeevaluateallmethodsusingasampledclosed-setsentenceidentificationprotocol. Foreach\nparadigm, the candidate pool C contains all unique sentences in the dataset, tokenized into\nCTC targets y . For each evaluation EEG trial (ground-truth y∗), we construct a candidate\ni\nsubset C containing one positive and K (=100 by default) negative candidates sampled\nsub\nfrom C\\{y∗}.\nWe report Top-1, Top-5, and Top-10 accuracies, Character Error Rate (CER), and\nCRA@1 (Chance-Ratio Accuracy at 1), defined as Top-1 accuracy divided by the random-\nchance baseline 1/(K+1); a CRA@1 of n× means the model is n times better than chance.\n4.3 Experimental setup\nAll models are trained on the same sentence-level splits and evaluated under the protocol\ndescribed in Section 4.2. This ensures that performance differences reflect model design\nrather than data splits or candidate construction.\nTable 5 in Appendix C.1 summarises the implementation of EEGAlign. Unless otherwise\nstated, the encoder refers to the default lightweight instantiation described in Section 3;\nthe alternative instantiation that replaces this encoder with a pretrained EEG foundation\nencodersharesallothercomponentsverbatimandisusedasasecondreferenceconfiguration.\n4.4 Baselines\nWecompareEEGAlignagainstthreeexternalbaselineschosentoreflectdifferentstrategiesfor\nstrengthening EEG decoding. LaBraM-MSE uses the pretrained LaBraM EEG foundation\nmodel[14]toencodeEEGandtrainsaprojectionheadwithanMSEobjectivetomatchfrozen\nBGE-M3 sentence embeddings. DeWave [4] is a representative EEG-to-text baseline based\non discrete neural tokenization and a pretrained language model; in our implementation, its\nBART component is replaced with a Chinese-adapted BART checkpoint. SMM-Challenge\nadaptstheEEG-Stimulus-Match-Mismatchmodel[22],whichcombinesself-supervisedspeech\nrepresentationsandcontextualtextembeddingsforstimulus-responsematching. Allbaselines\nare evaluated under our closed-set protocol using their own available ranking scores.1\n4.5 Main results\nWe evaluate EEGAlign against three baselines using two distinct backbones: a lightweight\nConformer and a pretrained LaBraM.\n1Resources for external baselines: DeWave implementation, Chinese BART checkpoint, and SMM-\nChallengecode.\n7\n=== PAGE 8 ===\nTable 1: Closed-set sentence identification with one positive and 100 negatives.\nRA(ReadingAloud) PL(PassiveListening)\nMethod Encoder\nTop-1 Top-5 Top-10 CRA@1 Top-1 Top-5 Top-10 CRA@1\nLaBraM-MSE[14] LaBraM-base 23.33 45.58 56.92 23.57× 19.44 43.84 57.02 19.64×\nDeWave[4] BART+VQ 1.73 6.86 15.19 1.75× 2.91 13.38 21.73 2.94×\nSMM-Challenge[22] Brainnetwork 26.22 70.90 89.55 26.48× 3.11 9.57 17.00 3.14×\nEEGAlign (ours) Conformer 69.62 91.15 94.04 70.31× 27.26 63.72 78.13 27.54×\nEEGAlign (ours) LaBraM-base 82.37 91.60 94.29 83.20× 41.43 79.79 91.09 41.84×\nAnalysis of Results. Table 1 presents the performance of EEGAlign. We highlight two\nkey insights:\n1)FrameworkEffectiveness: BothEEGAlignvariantsconsistentlyoutperformallbaselines\nbyalargemargin. Thefoundation-encoderversionachieves82.37%Top-1onRAand41.43%\non PL, demonstrating that dense auxiliary alignment to both text and audio is critical for\nrobust decoding.\n2) Encoder Robustness: Substantial gains are observed across both the lightweight Con-\nformer and the pretrained LaBraM backbone, confirming the improvements are framework-\nintrinsic. LaBraM’s extra capacity to preserve channel-level spatial information further\nconditions the representation, yielding additional +12.8 and +14.2 point boosts on RA and\nPL.\nAppendix E further reports a standard stratified five-fold RA cross-validation analysis,\nwith a mean Top-1 accuracy of 82.20% and a standard deviation of 1.68 percentage points.\n4.6 Ablation studies\nBuilding on the main results, we conduct ablation studies to isolate the contributions of\nEEGAlign’s individual components. All ablations use the 9.91M-parameter Conformer\nbackbone and the same closed-set protocol with K = 100 candidates as in Table 1. The\nvariantsinTable2followthetablenotation: w/o audioremovesaudioalignment, w/o text\nremovestextalignment,w/o bothremovesbothalignmentobjectives,w/o adapterremoves\nsubject-specific adapters, and w/o curriculum removes the staged training curriculum.\nTable 2: Alignment-component ablation on the Conformer encoder.\nRA (Reading Aloud) PL (Passive Listening)\nVariant\nTop-1 Top-5 Top-10 CER (%)↓ Top-1 Top-5 Top-10 CER (%)↓\nFull EEGAlign (text + audio) 69.62 91.15 94.04 28.90 27.26 63.72 78.13 68.30\nw/o audio 56.92 79.55 82.05 59.80 21.12 58.62 74.71 71.00\nw/o text 62.63 80.71 84.49 37.60 26.92 62.47 73.88 64.70\nw/o both 66.28 85.51 88.59 34.30 23.55 59.92 70.60 67.10\nw/o adapter 63.59 88.65 92.31 34.55 25.60 61.51 71.67 67.07\nw/o curriculum 67.05 89.74 93.91 31.30 25.54 60.47 72.08 67.67\nOverall, Table 2 shows that EEGAlign’s gains come from three interacting factors: joint\ntext–audio alignment, subject-specific adaptation, and progressive curriculum optimization.\n(i) The alignment objectives are complementary rather than independently additive. On\nRA, adding only one auxiliary axis is worse than using no auxiliary alignment: text-only\nalignment drops to 56.92 and audio-only alignment reaches 62.63, both below the CTC-only\nbaseline (66.28). In contrast, combining the two axes improves Top-1 to 69.62, the best\nresult among all variants. This non-monotonic pattern suggests that either text or audio\nsupervisionalonemayimposeanincompleteconstraintontheEEGencoder,whiletheirjoint\nuse regularizes the representation from complementary perspectives. (ii) The subject-specific\nadapters are also important: removing them reduces Top-1 from 69.62 to 63.59 on RA\nand from 27.26 to 25.60 on PL. This indicates that the lightweight adapters help absorb\n8\n=== PAGE 9 ===\ninter-subject variability without replacing the shared encoder. (iii) Finally, removing the\ncurriculum degrades both paradigms, indicating that progressive optimization is necessary\nto stabilize the heterogeneous CTC, text, and audio objectives.\n4.7 Analysis\nThis section provides a detailed analysis of the inference-time fusion gain, candidate-set\nsensitivity, andaudio-alignmentweightsensitivity. Wefurtherexaminewhetherthesemantic\nbranch reshapes the encoder representation itself, with complementary paradigm-specific\nanalyses deferred to Appendix G.\nFusion gain over CTC-only ranking. We compare EEGAlign’s default fused score\nsDA (Eq. 5, α=0.4) to the degenerate case of ranking candidates by the CTC log-likelihood\nonly, using the same trained model. Figure 2(a) shows the absolute Top-1 gain of fusion\nover CTC-only graphically (full numerical details are provided in Appendix B).\n80\n60\n40\n20\n0\nRA RA PL PL\nConformer LaBraM Conformer LaBraM\n)%(\nycarucca\n1-poT\n(a) Fusion gain over CTC-only\n100\nF\nC\nu\nT\ns\nC\ne\n-\nd\no n\n(\nly\n=0+.44).4\n+3.4 80\n60 +11.5\n40\n+1.6\n20\n0\n5 20 50 100 500\nCandidate-set size K\n)%(\nycarucca\n1-poT\n(b) Candidate-set sensitivity (LaBraM)\n85\n84\n83\n82\n81\n80 RA Top-1 P R L A T C o R p A -1 @1 79\nPL CRA@1\n78\na=0.5 a=0.1\n)%(\nycarucca\n1-poT\n(c) Audio-weight sensitivity (RA, LaBraM)\n400\nTop-1 (%)\n350 CER (%)\n300\n250 82.37 81.67 200\n150\n100 50\n0\n)×( 1@ARC\n20\n19\n18\n17\n16\n15\n)%( REC 17.82 17.26\nFigure 2: Analysis of fusion gain, hyperparameter sensitivity, and qualitative case studies.\n(a) Absolute Top-1 gain of fused score (α = 0.4) over CTC-only scoring; C and L denote\nConformer and LaBraM. (b) Candidate-set sensitivity for EEGAlign-LaBraM with varying\nnegativesizesK. (c)Sensitivitytotheaudio-alignmentweightλ onRA.(d)Qualitative\naudio\nUMAPvisualisation: EEGAlign(reddots)movesrepresentationsofCTC-onlybaseline(blue\ndots) toward ground-truth BGE-M3 embeddings (stars).\nTwo points are worth emphasising. (i) The fused score dominates the CTC-only score\nin every setting, confirming that the semantic embedding head and the CTC head provide\ncomplementary evidence at inference time. (ii) The magnitude of the gain depends strongly\non paradigm and encoder: the largest gain (+11.54) is on PL with the LaBraM encoder,\nwhere CTC evidence is weak and the frozen BGE-M3 anchor is useful as a corrective signal\n(Table 4); the smallest gains (+1.5 to +4.4) are on RA and on the Conformer variants,\nwhere the CTC branch is already near-saturated. This is consistent with the view that\ntext alignment functions mainly as a complementary inference-time score, rather than as a\nreplacement for the CTC score.\nCandidate-set sensitivity. BecausetheabsoluteTop-1accuracyofclosed-setidentifica-\ntion depends mechanically on the candidate-pool size K+1, we evaluate EEGAlign-LaBraM\non both RA and PL under five candidate sizes (K ∈{5,20,50,100,500}). As Figure 2(b)\nsummarises, RA Top-1 degrades gracefully from 93.21% at K=5 to 67.50% at K=500, while\nPL Top-1 drops from 86.32% to 16.38%. Crucially, CRA@1 over chance grows with K\nfor both paradigms. Although direct comparisons warrant caution due to differences in\nlanguageandstimuli,ourPLresultsgenerallyalignwith—and,underourclosed-setsentence-\nidentification protocol, outperform—representative auditory speech-perception decoding and\nICASSP challenge-style match-mismatch systems [20, 22]. Full per-K results are reported in\nAppendix C.2.\nAudio-weight sensitivity. We also examine the model’s sensitivity to the audio-\nalignment weight λ on RA. As shown in Figure 2(c), the default gentler audio-alignment\naudio\nweight (λ =0.1) yields slightly better Top-1 accuracy (82.37% vs. 81.67%) and lower\naudio\nCharacter Error Rate (17.26% vs. 17.82%) compared to the stronger setting λ =0.5,\naudio\n9\n=== PAGE 10 ===\nindicating that while acoustic supervision is crucial, it should not overpower the primary\nCTC objective.\nRepresentation-level structural alignment. The fusion analysis above demonstrates\nthat the semantic branch contributes useful inference-time evidence. We compute Centered\nKernelAlignment(CKA)[31]betweentheupsampledEEGencoderoutput(d=256,afterthe\nupsampler, before any projection head) and frozen BGE-M3 text embeddings (d=1024) on\n200 held-out RA evaluation trials. The aggregate CKA results are reported in Appendix H;\nin brief, EEGAlign achieves the highest structural alignment, increasing linear CKA from\n0.294 to 0.354 and RBF CKA from 0.332 to 0.402 relative to the CTC-only baseline, while\neach single-axis variant improves CKA only mildly. This supports the same conclusion as\nTable 2.\nFigure 2(d) gives a compact qualitative view of this effect on representative rescued cases.\nTheUMAPprojectionshowsthatEEGAlignmovestheCTC-onlybaselinetowardthecorrect\nBGE-M3 sentence anchor; the retained CKA summary and encoder-to-text representation\nmeasurements are provided in Appendices H and G.\nCase-level retrieval comparison. Beyondtheaggregatemetrics, weinspectindividual\nretrieval outcomes by examining the displayed case examples produced by EEGAlign, the\nCTC-only scoring baseline, and LaBraM-MSE (embedding-only retrieval without CTC). On\n10representativetrials(5RA,5PL)wherebothbaselinesfailatTop-1butEEGAlignsucceeds,\nthe CTC-only baseline ranks the ground-truth sentence at positions 20–92 (median ∼82)\nand LaBraM-MSE at positions 2–93, while EEGAlign places the correct sentence at rank 1\nin every case. This indicates that text-audio alignment leverages complementary evidence:\nthe alignment score and CTC log-likelihood provide strong sentence-level corrections to\neach other. Five cases for each paradigm and per-method rank comparisons are provided in\nAppendix I.1.\n5 Conclusion and limitations\nEEGAligninvestigateswhethertextandaudioalignmentprovidecomplementarysupervision\nfor Chinese EEG-to-text decoding in speech production and perception. Our results suggest\nthatsimultaneousEEG–textandEEG–speechalignmentyieldsstrongclosed-setclassification\nperformance under a 1-in-101 protocol and, crucially, does so across two distinct encoder\ninstantiations. The generic Conformer and the pretrained LaBraM achieve up to 69.62% and\n82.37% Top-1 on RA, respectively, outperforming all external baselines by large margins.\nThis consistent gain confirms the robustness of the text-audio alignment recipe, independent\nof the specific backbone.\nThe two alignment supervision signals are not interchangeable: audio alignment acts as\nthe dominant training-time auxiliary, while the text alignment head contributes primarily as\na complementary inference-time score, with the largest fusion gain (+11.5 Top-1) on PL,\nwhere the sequence-level signal is weak. A gentle audio-alignment weight (λ = 0.1)\naudio\nis sufficient and slightly preferable to a stronger one. Per-subject analysis (Appendix D)\nshows that all 12 subjects with reported values benefit substantially from full EEGAlign\nover CTC-only, with a tight spread across subjects (Top-1 std. ≤ 2.3 points on RA and\n≤1.7 points on PL for the best foundation-encoder variant). Together with the stratified\nfive-foldRAcross-validationresult(Top-182.20±1.68%;AppendixE),thesefindingsindicate\nthat the proposed alignment recipe is stable under the present closed-set, subject-aware\nevaluation. This evaluation setting, however, is still a constrained step toward practical\nEEG-to-text communication: the current decoder scores a fixed candidate set rather than\nperforming open-ended beam-search sentence decoding, and the subject-specific adapters\nrequire observed subject data rather than supporting zero-shot decoding for unseen users.\nMore broadly, the modest number of subjects and limited amount of data also constrain the\nintegration of EEG encoders with larger generative language decoders. Addressing these\nlimitations is a key step toward deployable non-invasive Chinese neural communication\nsystems.\n10\n=== PAGE 11 ===\nReferences\n[1] Francis R Willett, Erin M Kunz, Chaofei Fan, Donald T Avansino, Guy H Wilson, Eun Young\nChoi, Foram Kamdar, Matthew F Glasser, Leigh R Hochberg, Shaul Druckmann, et al. A\nhigh-performance speech neuroprosthesis. Nature, 620(7976):1031–1036, 2023.\n[2] Vinay Jayaram, Morteza Alamgir, Yasemin Altun, Bernhard Scholkopf, and Moritz Grosse-\nWentrup. Transfer learning in brain-computer interfaces. IEEE Computational Intelligence\nMagazine, 11(1):20–31, 2016.\n[3] Bo-Qun Ma, He Li, Wei-Long Zheng, and Bao-Liang Lu. Reducing the subject variability of\neeg signals with adversarial domain generalization. In International Conference on Neural\nInformation Processing, pages 30–42. Springer, 2019.\n[4] Yiqun Duan, Jinzhao Zhou, Zhen Wang, Yu-Kai Wang, and Chin-teng Lin. Dewave: Discrete\n", "numLines": 601, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 601}}}
