ntific Data, 11(1):550, 2024.\n[31] Simon Kornblith, Mohammad Norouzi, Honglak Lee, and Geoffrey Hinton. Similarity of neural\nnetwork representations revisited. In International conference on machine learning, pages\n3519–3529. PMlR, 2019.\n[32] Jacky Tai-Yu Lu, Jung Chiang, Chi-Sheng Chen, Anna Nai-Yun Tung, Hsiang Wei Hu, and\nYuan Chiao Cheng. Eeg2text-cn: An exploratory study of open-vocabulary chinese text-eeg\nalignment via large language model and contrastive learning on chineseeeg. arXiv preprint\narXiv:2506.00854, 2025.\n12\n=== PAGE 13 ===\nA Comparison with representative neural decoding meth-\nods\nTable 3 positions EEGAlign against representative methods across the design dimensions\nmost relevant to our setting.\nTable3: Representativeneuraldecodingmethods. CL:contrastivelearning; PLM:pretrained\nlanguage model; SR: Silent Reading; PL: Passive Listening; Overt: overt speech production.\nMethod Venue Lang. Paradigm Alignment Decoder Task\nstrategy\nDeWave[4] NeurIPS’23 EN SR VQ+PLM Pretrained EEG→text\nalignment LM\nE2T-PTR[5] ACL’24 EN SR CET-MAE BART EEG→text\ntransfer decoder\nNeuroLM[7] ICLR’25 mixed multi-task neuraltokenizer LLM EEG\n+instruction backbone multi-task\ntuning\nLLM-Guided[8] ESWA EN SR semantic LLM-guided EEG→text\nEEG-language decoder\nalignment\nEEG2TEXT- arXiv’25 CN SR text-EEGCL MiniLM/ EEG→text\nCN[32] dec.\nDéfossezetal.[20] NMI’23 EN/Dutch PL wav2vec2CL Retrieval MEG/EEG→\nspeechsegment\nWangetal.[22] ICASSPW’24 Dutch PL speech+text Match– EEG–stimulus\nCL mismatch matching\nConvConcatNet[12]ICASSPW’24 Dutch PL-like acousticrecon. CNN EEG→mel\nSSM2Mel[10] ICASSP’25 Dutch PL-like acousticrecon. SSM/Mamba EEG→mel\nLietal.[16] NER’23 CN Overt Riemannian LDA EEG→\nfeat. tone/vowel\n3M-CPSEED[17] SciData’26 CN Overt, Dataset — Pinyin\nmouthed, production\nimagined\nEEGAlign — CN Overt Text + audio CTC head EEG → text\n(RA) & PL\nB Fused versus CTC-only scoring\nTable4: Scoring-protocolcomparisononEEGAligncheckpoints(K =100). ∆istheabsolute\nTop-1 gain of fused scoring over CTC-only.\nSetting Encoder Fused Top-1 (%) CTC-only Top-1 (%) ∆\nRA, Full EEGAlign Conformer 69.62 66.22 +3.40\nRA, Full EEGAlign LaBraM 82.37 78.01 +4.36\nPL, Full EEGAlign Conformer 27.26 25.71 +1.55\nPL, Full EEGAlign LaBraM 41.43 29.89 +11.54\nC Implementation and sensitivity details\nThisappendixcollectsthreeitemsthatarereferencedfrombutnotdisplayedinthemaintext:\n(i) the full implementation summary (Table 5, Appendix C.1), (ii) the full set of closed-set\nmetrics across candidate-pool sizes K ∈{5,20,50,100,500} for EEGAlign-LaBraM on both\nRAandPL(Tables6–7,AppendixC.2),and(iii)thecompleteresultsfortheaudio-alignment\n13\n=== PAGE 14 ===\nweight sweep (Table 8, Appendix C.3). Each sub-appendix is a self-contained unit that\nreproduces the numbers that the main-text figures already show graphically.\nC.1 Implementation summary\nTable 5 summarises the full implementation of EEGAlign used for every experiment in\nSection4. EachrunusesasingleGPUandtakesapproximately16GPU-hours;peakmemory\nis approximately 5GB for the Conformer instantiation and 10GB for the LaBraM-based\ninstantiation. The reported experiments comprise roughly 20 independent runs, for an\nestimated total of about 320 GPU-hours; preliminary and debugging runs were modest\nrelative to this total. The alignment heads, subject adapters, upsampler, CTC decoder,\noptimiser, and curriculum are held fixed across the two reference instantiations; only the\nencoder module differs.\nTable 5: Implementation summary of EEGAlign. Only the encoder module is swapped when\ncomparing the two reference instantiations.\nComponent Configuration\nEncoder (default, from L = 3 Conformer [28], d = 256, H = 8, d = 512, conv kernel\nffn\nscratch) K =31\nEncoder (alternative, pre- LaBraM-base [14], frozen stem + fine-tuned top blocks\ntrained)\nSubject adapters Bottleneck residual at layers 0–1, d =32, zero-init up-projection\nb\nUpsampler ConvTranspose1D(kernel=stride=5) + LayerNorm\nText alignment Linear(d→1024) → frozen BGE-M3, InfoNCE, τ =0.05\nAudio alignment Linear(d→768) → wav2vec 2.0, bidirectional InfoNCE, chunk\nc=20\nCTC decoder Vocabulary |V|=21,128, beam width 10\nOptimizer AdamW, lr=2×10−4, weight decay 10−2, grad clip 5.0\nBatch size 32 (PL) / 16 (RA); warmup = 4000; cosine annealing\nCompute Single GPU; ∼5GB peak memory for Conformer, ∼10GB for\nLaBraM, ∼16 GPU-hours per run\nTotal parameters (de- ∼9.91M + 16.4K × subjects\nfault encoder)\nC.2 Candidate-set sensitivity: full results\nFigure 2(b) in the main text plots the Top-1 and CRA@1 columns of this experiment.\nTables 6–7 report the full metrics for the foundation-encoder (LaBraM) EEGAlign on RA\nand PL respectively.\nTable 6: Candidate-set sensitivity for closed-set retrieval (EEGAlign-LaBraM on RA, seed =\n42). Random Top-1 is 1/(K+1).\nK Top-1 (%) Top-5 (%) Top-10 (%) CER (%) Random Top-1 (%) CRA@1\n5 93.21 99.74 100.00 7.06 16.67 5.59×\n20 89.49 96.67 98.97 10.87 4.76 18.79×\n50 85.96 93.40 96.15 13.99 1.96 43.84×\n100 82.37 91.60 94.29 17.26 0.99 83.20×\n500 67.50 86.67 88.97 31.20 0.20 338.18×\n14\n=== PAGE 15 ===\nTable 7: Candidate-set sensitivity for closed-set retrieval (EEGAlign-LaBraM on PL, seed =\n42). Random Top-1 is 1/(K+1).\nK Top-1 (%) Top-5 (%) Top-10 (%) CER (%) Random Top-1 (%) CRA@1\n5 86.32 100.00 100.00 15.32 16.67 5.18×\n20 69.11 97.10 99.76 33.04 4.76 14.51×\n50 53.90 89.50 96.30 48.65 1.96 27.49×\n100 41.43 79.79 91.09 61.61 0.99 41.84×\n500 16.38 49.03 65.10 85.71 0.20 82.06×\nC.3 Audio-alignment weight sensitivity: full results\nFigure 2(c) in the main text visualises the Top-1 and CER columns of this sweep. Table 8\nreports the remaining metrics.\nTable 8: Audio-alignment weight sensitivity (foundation-encoder EEGAlign on RA).\nAudio weight λ Top-1 (%) Top-5 (%) Top-10 (%) CER (%)↓ ctc_top1 (%)\naudio\n0.5 (stronger) 81.67 92.12 94.74 17.82 78.97\n0.1 (default) 82.37 91.60 94.29 17.26 78.01\nD Per-subject results\nTables 9–10 and Figure 3 report the per-subject Top-1 accuracy (closed-set, K =100, seed\n= 42) at the best evaluation epoch for each training configuration, extracted directly from\nthe training logs (the per-subject breakdown is emitted alongside every eval1/101 line as\n[eval][per_subject] sub-X top1=...n=...).\nThree observations are worth highlighting. (i) The LaBraM + text-audio alignment\nmodel dominates all four RA subjects and all eight PL subjects uniformly: the subject-wise\nminimum on the best RA configuration (λ =0.1) is 80.20% (sub-m1), still +8 points\naudio\nabovethebestConformersubject, andthePLminimumis39.50%(sub-06), alsoaboveevery\nConformer subject. (ii) The per-subject spread is small (RA std. 2.06 points, PL std. 1.67\npoints for the best LaBraM variant), indicating that the reported aggregate accuracy is\nnot driven by one or two outlier subjects. (iii) The Conformer ablations reveal a paradigm-\ndependent effect of the auxiliary objectives: on RA, the full model is best for every subject\nwhile CTC-only remains stronger than either single-axis variant; on PL, the audio-only\nvariant is the second-best aggregate configuration and improves over CTC-only for every\nlisted subject, consistent with the weaker sequence-level signal in passive listening.\nTable 9: Per-subject Top-1 (%) on RA. n is the number of evaluation trials per subject.\nConfiguration sub-f1 sub-f2 sub-m1 sub-m2 Mean Std\n(n=382) (n=385) (n=394) (n=399)\nFull EEGAlign (LaBraM, λ =0.1) 81.15 84.68 80.20 83.46 82.37 2.06\naudio\nFullEEGAlign(LaBraM,λ =0.5) 81.94 84.16 78.17 82.46 81.67 2.51\naudio\nFullEEGAlign(Conformer) 64.14 70.39 71.07 72.68 69.62 3.75\nw/oaudio(Conformer) 54.45 57.92 56.60 58.65 56.92 1.86\nw/otext(Conformer) 56.81 64.42 63.71 65.58 62.63 3.96\nw/oboth(Conformer) 60.47 68.57 67.51 68.42 66.28 3.87\n15\n=== PAGE 16 ===\n90\n85\n80\n75\n70\n65\n60\n55\n50\n45\nsub-f1 sub-f2 sub-m1 sub-m2\n)%(\nycarucca\n1-poT\n(a) RA (4 subjects)\n50\n45\n40\n35\n30\n25\nFull DualAlign (LaBraM, =0.1)\nFull DualAlign (LaBraM, =0.5)\nFull DualAlign (Conformer) 20\nw/o audio (Conformer)\nw/o both (CTC only, Conformer)\n15\nsub-01 sub-02 sub-03 sub-04 sub-05 sub-06 sub-07 sub-08\n)%(\nycarucca\n1-poT\n(b) PL (8 subjects)\nFull DualAlign (LaBraM)\nFull DualAlign (Conformer)\nw/o audio (Conformer)\nw/o both (CTC only, Conformer)\nFigure 3: Per-subject Top-1 accuracy. (a) RA (4 subjects). (b) PL (8 subjects). Dashed\nlines mark the across-subject mean for EEGAlign-LaBraM.\nTable 10: Per-subject Top-1 (%) on PL. n is the number of evaluation trials per subject.\nConfiguration sub-01sub-02sub-03sub-04sub-05sub-06sub-07sub-08 Mean Std\nFull EEGAlign (LaBraM) 42.65 40.12 39.84 42.58 43.85 39.50 41.29 41.3441.431.57\nFullEEGAlign(Conformer) 28.31 28.10 25.33 29.33 28.28 24.16 27.38 27.19 27.26 1.79\nw/oaudio(Conformer) 22.71 21.31 20.78 20.79 20.23 19.21 22.66 21.27 21.12 1.16\nw/otext(Conformer) 25.37 28.85 28.80 28.13 25.90 25.73 25.47 26.50 26.92 1.56\nw/oboth(Conformer) 23.65 21.77 23.76 23.81 21.60 25.02 25.21 23.57 23.55 1.31\nE Five-fold cross-validation\nAll main-text results (Tables 1–2) use a single fixed 90/10 split (seed = 42). To assess\nsplit sensitivity, we additionally run standard stratified five-fold cross-validation for the\nLaBraM-based RA model. The five evaluation folds are mutually exclusive and together\ncover the RA trials; in each fold, 80% of the data are used for training and the remaining\n20% for evaluation.\nTable 11 reports the per-fold closed-set metrics. The single fixed-split result from Table 1\nis reproduced in the last row for reference, but should not be interpreted as one of the five\ncross-validation folds.\nTable 11: Five-fold cross-validation results of EEGAlign-LaBraM on RA.\nTop-1 (%) Top-5 (%) Top-10 (%) CER (%)↓\nFold 1 80.55 89.10 95.20 16.00\nFold 2 83.90 92.45 93.15 21.00\nFold 3 81.20 91.80 94.80 17.50\nFold 4 84.15 89.95 92.90 19.50\nFold 5 81.20 91.05 94.20 17.50\nMean 82.20 90.87 94.05 18.30\nStd 1.68 1.31 0.96 1.90\nFixed split (Table 1) 82.37 91.60 94.29 17.26\nF Paradigm transfer analysis\n16\n=== PAGE 17 ===\nRA\nPL\nRA PL\nEvaluation paradigm\nmgidarap\ngniniarT\n(a) 200-way Top-1 transfer\n0.09\n0.08\n24.0\\% 0.5\\% 0.07\n0.06\n0.05\n0.04\n0.03\n1.5\\% 6.0\\% 0.02\n0.01\n0.00\nRA → RA RA → PL PL → RA PL → PL\n−soc−+soc=Δ\nnigram\nenisoc\n(b) Paradigm-specific alignment\n0.30\n0.25 0.0691\n0.20\n0.0513\n0.15\n0.10\n0.05\n0.0072 0.0050\n0.00\nFigure 4: Cross-paradigm transfer analysis. (a) 200-way in-batch Top-1 using the text\nhead only, for every (training paradigm, evaluation paradigm) pair. Diagonal cells are the\nvalues reported in Figure 5; off-diagonal cells collapse to near chance. (b) Cosine margin\n∆=cos+−cos− for the same four pairs, showing the same ordering of magnitude. Cross-\nparadigm alignment is essentially destroyed, supporting the paradigm-specific interpretation\nin Appendix G.\nG Encoder-to-text representation analysis\nHow do the learned RA and PL encoder outputs align with text? Beyond the inference-time\nfusion gain, we directly compare the sentence-level EEG representations (e¯ ) from each\neeg\nparadigm to the frozen BGE-M3 ground-truth text embeddings. Evaluated on 200 held-out\nevaluation trials per paradigm (seed = 42) using the shared encoder bypass, we report two\nviews: (i) cosine similarity between e¯ and text embeddings for matched vs. mismatched\neeg\npairs, and (ii) the 200-way text-only retrieval score (no CTC fusion). Table 12 reports both\nviews, separately for the RA and PL checkpoints; Figure 5 summarises them graphically.\nAll four panels of Figure 5 place the RA encoder (on RA data) and the PL encoder (on PL\ndata) side by side, so the figure is a direct “RA encoder vs. PL encoder” comparison of the\nencoder output itself.\nTable 12: Encoder-feature text-alignment measurements on 200 held-out evaluation trials\nper paradigm.\nTrain paradigm Eval paradigm cos pos − cos neg 200-way Top-1 (%) 200-way MRR\nRA-trained RA (same) 0.0691 24.00 0.367\nPL-trained PL (same) 0.0513 6.00 0.127\nRA-trained PL (cross) 0.0072 0.50 0.027\nPL-trained RA (cross) 0.0050 1.50 0.043\nFour observations follow from Table 12 and Figure 5. (i) Both paradigms yield encoders\nwhosesentence-levelrepresentationsaremeasurablyalignedwithBGE-M3: thecosinemargin\nis +0.069 on RA and +0.051 on PL, and the 200-way text-only Top-1 is 48× / 12× above\nchance. So the text projection learns a non-trivial text-alignment on both RA and PL, not\non RA alone. (ii) The alignment is nevertheless clearly stronger on RA: the margin is ∼35%\nlarger, the 200-way Top-1 is four times higher (24.0% vs. 6.0%), and the median rank on the\nfull 4,000+ candidate gallery is roughly three times better (∼2% vs. ∼5.7% of the gallery).\nThisisconsistentwithRA’sstrongersequence-levelCTCsignal: awell-decodedCTCbranch\nmakes the sentence-level pooled embedding a more reliable proxy for text semantics. (iii)\nThe alignment is paradigm-specific: when we evaluate the RA encoder on PL data (or\nvice versa, Appendix F), the margin collapses by an order of magnitude (≤ 0.007) and\n200-way retrieval drops to 0.5–1.5%, i.e. essentially chance. This justifies training separate\nRA and PL encoders rather than a single multi-paradigm model. (iv) The ordering “RA >\n17\n=== PAGE 18 ===\n0.15\n0.10\n0.05 0.00\n−0.05\nRA encoder PL encoder\n→ RA data → PL data\nenisoc\n3M-EGB\n↔ GEE\n(a) Text alignment strength\np ne os g i a ti t v iv e e ( m (m a i t s c m he a d tc ) hed)Δ=+0.051 5 Δ=+0.069 4\n3\n2 1\n0\n−0.1 0.0 0.1 0.2 0.3\nper-trial cosine margin cos+−cos−\nytisned\n(b) Margin distribution (approx.)\nmmeaena=n0=.00.50169 RA encoder 70 PL encoder 60 50\n40\n30 20 10\nchance 0\nTop-1 Top-5 Top-10\n)%( laveirter\nylno-citnameS\n(c) 200-way retrieval (no CTC)\n0.5 R PL A e e n n c c o o d d e e r r → → P R L A 61.5% chance k/20 5 0 0.5% 0.4\n0.3\n24.0% 0.2 23.5% 16.5% 0.1\n6.0% 0.0\nRA encoder PL encoder\n→ RA data → PL data\nRRM\n(d) Summary alignment metrics\n10 MRR median rank (\\% of gallery) 0.367 8\n6\n4 0.127 2\n0\n)yrellag\nfo %\\(\nknar naidem\n5.7\\%\n2.0\\%\nFigure 5: Encoder-feature text-alignment analysis for RA and PL paradigms. (a) Mean\nEEG–BGE-M3 cosine on matched vs. mismatched pairs. (b) Per-trial margin distribution.\n(c) 200-way retrieval curves using only the text head. (d) 200-way MRR and median rank.\nThe cross-paradigm transfer matrix is in Appendix F.\nPL on alignment quality” matches the ordering “RA > PL on closed-set Top-1” (Table 1)\nbut not the ordering “PL > RA on fusion gain” (Table 4). These two orderings are not\ncontradictory: aweakerbutstilldiscriminativesemanticchannelcancontributemorerelative\nimprovement on top of a noisier CTC branch than a stronger text score can on top of an\nalready near-saturated CTC branch, which is exactly what we observe.\nWe keep the representation analysis compact and non-redundant: Table 12 gives the\nexact numerical measurements, Figure 5 provides the corresponding graphical summary,\nand Appendix F isolates the cross-paradigm transfer question. The larger diagnostic panels\nwith additional t-SNE/PCA projections, embedding-norm histograms, and per-channel\nvariance plots were used as sanity checks, but are omitted here because they repeat the same\nconclusion without changing the interpretation.\nH CKA alignment of encoder outputs\nSection 4.7 summarises the main CKA findings in text and uses the qualitative visualisation\nin Figure 2(d). This appendix provides the measurement protocol, exact numerical values,\nand one non-redundant four-condition CKA figure.\nProtocol. We compute CKA between the upsampled EEG encoder output (enc_up,\nd=256, after the ConvTranspose1D upsampler, before any projection head) and the frozen\nBGE-M3 sentence embeddings (d=1024) using 200 held-out RA evaluation trials (seed =42).\nWe report both linear-kernel CKA and RBF-kernel CKA (σ set by the median heuristic).\nBecauseCKAisdimension-agnosticandthecomparisonismadebeforeanyalignmentheadis\napplied,theresultreflectstheinternalencodergeometryandnotanartefactoftheprojection\ntraining.\nResults. Table 13 reports all four conditions.\nTable13: CKAbetweenEEGencoderoutput(enc_up)andfrozenBGE-M3textembeddings\non 200 held-out RA evaluation trials. Top-1 is the fused closed-set score at K=100 for\nreference.\nCondition Linear CKA RBF CKA Top-1 (%)\nCTC-only baseline 0.294 0.332 66.28\n+Text CL only 0.315 0.352 56.92\n+Audio CL only 0.321 0.353 62.63\nFull EEGAlign 0.354 0.402 69.62\nThree observations follow. (i) Full EEGAlign achieves the highest CKA on both kernels,\ndemonstratingthatjointtext-audiosupervisionshapestheencodergeometryandnotjustthe\nfinal scoring rule. (ii) Each single-axis variant improves CKA modestly over the CTC-only\n18\n=== PAGE 19 ===\nbaseline but does not jointly maximise CKA and Top-1 accuracy: notably, the text-CL-only\ncondition reaches Linear CKA 0.315 yet exhibits the lowest Top-1 (56.92%), suggesting that\ntext alignment alone may distort the CTC-useful acoustic geometry. (iii) The Full EEGAlign\ncondition aligns both objectives: its CKA improvement (+0.060 linear, +0.070 RBF over\nCTC-only) is accompanied by the top closed-set Top-1. We interpret this as evidence that\nthetwoalignmentheadsactasmutualregularisers,eachpreventingtheotherfromcollapsing\nthe encoder representation into a subspace that sacrifices the complementary signal.\nFigure 6 shows the single CKA visualisation retained in the appendix, combining the\nlinear/RBF measurements, their relation to Top-1 accuracy, and a compact bar summary in\none panel.\nFigure 6: CKA alignment analysis across all four ablation conditions. (a) Linear CKA\nbetween enc_up and BGE-M3 embeddings. (b) RBF CKA. (c) CKA vs. Top-1 scatter\nacross conditions, showing that Full EEGAlign is the unique Pareto-optimal point. (d)\nCompactbarsummary. Allmeasurementsuse200held-outRAevaluationtrials; theencoder\noutput is taken before any projection head.\nI Qualitative rescued-case analysis\nTo understand how EEGAlign corrects severe decoding failures, we inspect RA evaluation\ntrials where the CTC-only LaBraM baseline produces degenerate repetitive outputs while\nEEGAlign exactly recovers the target sentence. Under the strict criterion of CTC-only\nCER =1.0 and EEGAlign CER =0.0, we identify 63 candidate rescued cases among the\n1,560 held-out RA evaluation trials. The ten cases shown here were selected to cover all\nfour subjects (sub-f1, sub-f2, sub-m1, sub-m2) and a range of sentence lengths; no criterion\ninvolving the cosine values was applied.\nAcrosstheseexamples,themeancosinesimilarityis−0.030fortheCTC-onlybaselineand\n+0.259forEEGAlign,anaveragegainof+0.289. Thisshowsthatforeverydisplayedcasethe\n19\n=== PAGE 20 ===\ntext branch moves the EEG representation substantially closer to the correct sentence-level\nanchor, consistent with the aggregate CKA improvement reported in Appendix H.\nA representative UMAP projection is shown in Figure 2(d) in the main text. The\npanel shows the ground-truth BGE-M3 embedding (star), the CTC-only EEG embedding\n(circle), and the EEGAlign EEG embedding (triangle), illustrating how EEGAlign moves the\nrepresentationtowardthecorrectsentence-leveltextanchor. The2Dprojectionisqualitative;\naggregate quantitative alignment is reported in Table 13.\nThese examples are not used for model selection and are provided solely as qualitative\nevidence for the mechanism suggested by the aggregate fusion and CKA analyses: EEGAlign", "stderr": "\nShell cwd was reset to /inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
